shared date-keyed filename; fixed by giving each mode its own output path | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] | Handoff with `memlint.py` and four supporting files left uncommitted by choice, a temp-path placement recurrence, and a cross-session \"another session may have fixed it\" check | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/always-loaded-context-is-the-expensive-place-to-be-wrong]] | Wrongness, staleness, and volume are three costs on the same session-resident surface; consolidation concentrates the first unless the pointer's target is re-verified | 2026-09-02.md, 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/microsoft-forms-vs-google-forms-inventory-mismatch]] | Live inventory survey runs on Microsoft Forms while project docs point at 08-13-era Google Forms; a `gws` query there returns a truthful-but-false \"no new responses\", exposed only by the raw `.xlsx` header row | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/tier-a-memory-as-the-durable-payload-of-a-verification-session]] | A verification check's payload is a trap plus a command, so it routes to Tier A memory rather than a handoff doc; reinforced by the project not being a git repo | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/a-stale-pointer-resolves-cleanly-to-the-wrong-answer]] | Stale Google Form IDs, a stale DEV memory router, and a nearly-dangling AGENTS.md pointer all succeed at resolution while returning the wrong answer; check from the target's side and stamp a verification date | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] | Adds the unpushed `dc54c97`/`38e462b` commits and the cross-link to `DexaProject` still living as a public GitHub repo with a patient CSV | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] | Adds the 78→86 job-count drift from 2026-09-15, restating the tell in its threshold-free form (single-digit okCount = SSD absent) | 2026-09-09.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] | A `cp` to a date-keyed archive path destroyed three prior `.errors.log` lines (recovered from git); archive names must not collide or must append; `cp /dev/null` is the only working truncation route | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/session-transcript-tracking-repo-growth]] | Tracking session transcripts added ~368 MB to `~/.claude` and made repo size grow daily; left as an open decision because Tier B capture is a proven reconstruction fallback | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] | Archive-before-clear and free-run-overwrites-paid both failed by encoding a once-daily assumption into a filename; the verified tarball discipline shows why an unchecked safety net is worse than none | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] | `~/DEV` converted to thin repo `erash11/DEV` tracking 12 router files; `/*` block-all `.gitignore` as the load-bearing line, `CLAUDE.md` deliberately kept as a workspace map, Windows box confirmed active | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] | 21 public repos read inside: only `B_Ellis` and `cmj-norms-app` held real athlete data; 404-as-live bug and a Vercel-not-Pages miss in the liveness check; 18 flipped private | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] | 36 Windows/Mac conflicts collapse to 4 (26 CRLF-only, 6 Mac-newer generated); \"Mac wins\" would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] | Repo names, conflict flags, and cleaned exports all over- and under-include at once; treat the cheap list as a work queue, classify by kind before resolving | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] | Revisions supersede, accumulating records diverge; the correct verb is merge or append, and a smaller newer copy is the tell | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] | ~200 `.from-*` conflicts traced to a Windows save command mangling encoding and prepending BOMs; proved on `portfolio.json` (9 updates matched, nothing lost), root cause fixed, 95 BOM files left alone | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-oom-kill-during-first-time-index]] | Backup OOM-killed during a 20-min first-time index of `dev-docs` (547 files); index stale not corrupt, two dead-PID locks cleared, CPU% rejected as a health signal | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm]] | `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` was a symlink, not a missing repo; backup taught to skip symlinks, two real gaps added with a 0 lost / 0 changed / 2 added proof | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] | Four \"not a repo\" folders were parent-tracked, five \"missing\" repos were name mismatches, two `repo-diverged` errors had self-cleared; verify before `gh repo create` | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision]] | `ZoteroInjestion` made a real submodule but pinned to `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` rather than `main`; a snapshot pin decays silently, decision deferred | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] | Mangled sync saves, a symlink-blind manifest check, and a backup committing its own output: recurrence is the tell that the fix belongs in the generator | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/a-killed-process-leaves-debris-that-blocks-the-retry]] | Dead-PID locks and orphaned indexers are not self-healing; a loud crash becomes an indefinitely quiet no-op unless the kill's leavings are enumerated | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] | Multi-task Mac+Windows skills/backup plan: 42 own vs 19 third-party skills reframing, Task 4 inversion script deleted, Task 2 prune 306→208 as a reversible move, three open round-10 findings | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]] | A `.gitignore` pattern ending in `/` matches directories but not symlinks, so the rule silently no-ops and an automated backup re-adds the entries; plus `.gitignore` vs `.git/info/exclude` scope | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/backup-verification-by-restore]] | Restore test returned 57 of 61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain into an unbacked-up `~/.agents/vendor-review/`; only an actual restore can measure coverage the backup's own records cannot | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/cross-provider-review-gate]] | A rate-limit-forced switch from Codex to Claude found the plan's biggest error; resume cannot cross providers, and a prior approval is stale after an amendment (5 new bugs in the amendment itself) | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]] | Submodule conversion turned `.git` from directory to file; rsync cannot overwrite a destination directory with a file, failing only while the SSD is mounted; fixed by rename, `okCount: 80` | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror]] | Adds the Codex-skills symlink refusal as a second rsync type-change instance, making recurrence the argument for fixing the tooling | 2026-09-12.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[connections/change-the-checker-down-or-sideways]] | Replacing a failed check with a primitive substrate works only where a ground truth exists; otherwise the only lever is a peer with uncorrelated blind spots. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] | Session lifetime and mount state both silently shrink what a run attempts without producing an error, and both are fixed by moving the verdict onto a durable artifact that publishes cardinality. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/test-the-layer-before-blaming-the-artifact]] | A failed upload or render usually indicts the transport layer, not the file — hold the artifact fixed and remove a stage before rebuilding anything | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] | Windows laptop had no working backup: ~15 GB single-copy, nine Disabled tasks, two triggerless ones, and the Mac's manifest installed as Windows'; `/MIR`→`/E` fix | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] | Four Task Scheduler gotchas: `schtasks /Change` can't add a schedule, triggerless tasks show `Ready` forever, `G:` is per-user so SYSTEM writes nothing, sleeping laptops miss fixed-time triggers | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]] | Partition cross-machine repair by capability: author all file edits from the working machine, hand the broken one a paste-ready instruction page and in-file \"nothing reads this yet\" notes | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report]] | A received recovery report's findings held but one explanation didn't: `dev-sync` skips an enumerated list, not underscore-prefixed folders, so `_INBOX` was already a clean pushed repo | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions]] | Three Windows pushes mid-session each needing a merge, including a deliberate-looking 913-file skill audit cutting ~160→63; accepted because git retains deletions, intent still to be confirmed | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] | `Ready`, `status: OK`, and a backup's own records were each locally truthful while coverage failed; narrow stage predicates don't compose into end-to-end assurance | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory]] | Codex skills converted to symlinks while the SSD held real directories; rsync cannot replace a directory with a symlink, fixed by moving four folders to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/manifest-classification-not-inferable-from-names]] | Six Baylor-sounding repos (`VALD_Performance`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, …) were personal, not work; manifest classification must be asked, not inferred | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] | `.errors.log` archived and cleared only after a verified 0-error/86-job re-run — fixes are not their own confirmation | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/vendored-copy-drift-within-a-repo]] | `LibraryProject`'s embedded `ZoteroInjestion` one commit behind the standalone repo; `STATUS.md` missing its Next section with an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n\n\n## Related Wiki Articles\n\n### concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Windows Laptop Backup Failure and RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md\"\naliases: [RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md, Windows no working backup, ~15 GB single copy, nine disabled KeystoneOS tasks, Mac manifest on Windows, FIX-WINDOWS-BACKUP.md, backup_dev.ps1 MIR to E]\ntags: [backup, windows, macos, automation, keystoneos, scheduling, data-integrity, migration]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-14.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-14\nupdated: 2026-09-14\n---\n\n# Windows Laptop Backup Failure and RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md\n\nOn 2026-09-14 a `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` commit arriving from the Windows laptop revealed that the machine had **no working backup at all**. Roughly 15 GB exists in a single copy only — `SportScienceResearchRepo` (4.37 GB), `Football` (2.94 GB), `Risk_Stratification_Engine` (1.80 GB), `ForcePlate_DecisionSystem` (1.69 GB), and the `~/.codex` history. Everything else — all 131 repos, `~/.claude`, skills, global memory, and Google Drive documents — is safe. The failure had three independent root causes, all of them artifacts of the Mac migration.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Nine KeystoneOS Windows scheduled tasks are Disabled** — switched off during the move to the Mac and never restored. They report their disabled state accurately; nothing ever escalated it.\n- **Two replacement tasks were registered with no trigger.** A triggerless task shows `Ready` indefinitely and will never fire — the healthiest-looking possible state for a job that does nothing.\n- **The Windows backup manifest is the Mac's manifest.** It carries `/Volumes/CRASHBOX` and `/Users/kristinarash/...` paths, with its last state written 2026-08-14 at status ERROR. `~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1` had likewise been overwritten by the Mac version (rsync + Mac paths) on 2026-08-05; the original is frozen at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`.\n- **`backup_dev.ps1` was changed from `/MIR` to `/E`.** Mirroring propagates accidental deletions into the backup, which converts the backup into a second copy of a mistake rather than a recovery path. (`backup_ai_sessions.ps1` was already `/E`.)\n- **Two deliberate non-actions:** the backup scripts stay in `DEV\\_INBOX\\` because the registered scheduled task points there, and the nine disabled tasks are *not* re-enabled because the script they invoke is now the Mac's.\n\n## Details\n\nWhat makes this a distinct failure rather than another manifest-drift instance is that every layer reported truthfully and none of them reported the operational fact. A disabled task is disabled; a triggerless task is genuinely ready; a manifest containing Mac paths is a syntactically valid manifest. The only signal that would have surfaced the problem is the one nobody was checking: whether bytes actually landed in Google Drive. This is the same scope mismatch catalogued in [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]], where `launchctl list` reports only the last *scheduled* run — the field answers a narrower predicate than the operator's question and is therefore silent about the gap. The recovery report's own conclusion states the rule directly: verification must confirm bytes at the destination, not that the task looks healthy.\n\nThe three causes also share a single origin. Tasks disabled \"during the Mac move,\" a manifest overwritten with Mac paths on 08-14, a `daily-backup.ps1` overwritten on 08-05 — the migration edited the Windows machine's automation surface as a side effect of syncing shared files, and nothing on the Windows side was left running to notice. This extends the pattern in [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] in the opposite direction: that article covers Windows assumptions surviving onto the Mac, whereas here Mac-shaped configuration flowed back onto Windows through the same shared repositories. Bidirectional sync between two machines with platform-specific automation means any file that encodes host facts — paths, mount points, shell dialects — is a collision waiting to happen, which is why the new `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.windows.json` was created as a *separate* file rather than a re-edit of the shared one.\n\nThe `/MIR` → `/E` change deserves isolation as policy. Robocopy's `/MIR` makes the destination an exact mirror of the source, which means a local deletion is faithfully reproduced in the backup at the next run — the backup is then a copy of the current state rather than a record of history. `/E` copies without deleting, accepting stale files in exchange for never propagating a loss. The trade-off resolves the same way as the append-only forecast log in [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]]: when the artifact's value is that it holds something the source might no longer have, the correct verb is never \"make identical.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] — The four scheduling mechanics that make a Windows backup task silently ineffective, and the paste-ready fix page.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — `Ready`, `Disabled`, and a valid-but-wrong manifest all report accurately on a narrower predicate than \"is the data backed up?\"\n- [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] — The mirror-image case: platform-specific configuration surviving a migration, here flowing Mac → Windows.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The Mac-side manifest whose paths ended up on the Windows machine.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The companion lesson that a backup's own status field cannot establish coverage.\n- [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] — A triggerless task and a SYSTEM-context missing `G:` both shrink the work set without producing an error.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The Mac-side SSD leg; its `/backup` run was still outstanding at session close.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-14.md]] — Memory maintenance session (22:05): `RECOVERY-INVENTORY.md` arriving from the Windows laptop; ~15 GB in single copy only (SportScienceResearchRepo 4.37 GB, Football 2.94 GB, Risk_Stratification_Engine 1.80 GB, ForcePlate_DecisionSystem 1.69 GB, `~/.codex` history) with 131 repos, `~/.claude`, skills, global memory, and Drive docs safe; three root causes (nine Disabled KeystoneOS tasks, two triggerless replacement tasks showing `Ready`, Windows manifest actually the Mac's with `/Volumes/CRASHBOX` and `/Users/kristinarash/...` paths at status ERROR 2026-08-14); `backup_dev.ps1` changed `/MIR` → `/E`; `backup-manifest.windows.json` created as documentation only; scripts kept in `DEV\\_INBOX\\` and the nine tasks left disabled because `daily-backup.ps1` was overwritten by the Mac version on 2026-08-05 (original at `daily-backup.ps1.win-orig`).\n\n```\n\n### concepts/machine-restore-closeout-handoff.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Machine-Restore Closeout and Handoff Note\"\naliases: [restore closeout, 2026-08-06-restore-closeout, handoff note, global-memory handoffs, restore checklist, parked items, compiler backlog 54 logs, zotero MCP registration]\ntags: [workflow, machine-restore, handoff, second-brain, claude-code, project-management]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-11\nupdated: 2026-08-11\n---\n\n# Machine-Restore Closeout and Handoff Note\n\nThe 2026-08-11 end-of-day session closed out a multi-day machine-restore checklist: an SSD backup baseline, a Zotero CLI/MCP bundle installation, cleanup of a failed-run artifact, and a written handoff note committed to the global-memory repository. The closeout distinguishes between items genuinely finished, items intentionally left to drain on their own schedule, and items deliberately parked.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Handoff artifact:** `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-08-06-restore-closeout.md`, committed and pushed — a durable, versioned record of restore state rather than a memory-tier entry.\n- **Zotero MCP registered user-scope:** binary at `~/go/bin/zotero-research-library-pp-mcp`, registered as `zotero`; bundle at `~/printing-press/library/zotero-research-library/zotero-research-library.mcpb`.\n- **Backup baseline closed:** first full `CRASHBOX` run completed clean (OK, 0 errors, 21:25).\n- **Self-draining items are not blockers.** The compiler backlog of 54 daily logs drains on the nightly schedule; PR monitors for #1667 and #1668 were intentionally left stopped because the ball is on the maintainer's side.\n- **Parked items carried forward unchanged:** `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD`, Milvus, and skill-tax — the same three items parked during the 2026-08-06 loose-ends sweep, confirming they remain deliberate deferrals rather than forgotten work.\n\n## Details\n\nThe closeout illustrates a three-way classification that keeps an end-of-day review honest. The first class is genuinely complete work with a verifiable artifact: the backup ran clean and produced a receipt, the MCP binary was registered and its bundle staged, and the handoff note was committed and pushed. The second class is work that is neither done nor blocking because a scheduled process will drain it without attention — the 54-log compiler backlog is the canonical example, since the nightly compile job consumes it incrementally, so listing it as an action item would misrepresent it as requiring human effort. The third class is work owned by someone else or explicitly deferred: PRs #1667 and #1668 sit with the maintainer, so the background monitors watching them were stopped rather than left running to generate noise.\n\nThe parked trio — `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD`, Milvus, and skill-tax — is notable for having been carried unchanged since the 2026-08-06 sweep. Restating them at closeout rather than dropping them preserves the distinction between a deliberate deferral and an item that quietly fell off the list; a parked item that stops appearing in closeouts becomes indistinguishable from forgotten work. Routing the whole picture into a committed handoff file under `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/` rather than into a memory tier reflects the same routing discipline documented elsewhere: durable state that already has a natural on-disk home should live there, with memory reserved for pointers and conventions. Stopping the PR monitors also aligns with the finding that background monitor events are unreliable — a monitor left running against work that cannot progress produces duplicate or spurious notifications rather than signal.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The backup leg of the same closeout; its clean first baseline was one of the three completion criteria.\n- [[concepts/launchd-backup-receipt-race-repo-reconciliation]] — The 2026-08-06 loose-ends sweep that originally parked `GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD`, Milvus, and skill-tax and left repos pending reconciliation.\n- [[concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability]] — Rationale for leaving the #1667/#1668 monitors stopped rather than running them against maintainer-owned work.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The content-to-tier routing logic that puts a committed handoff file on disk rather than duplicating restore state into a memory tier.\n- [[concepts/zotero-research-library-pp-cli]] — The Zotero CLI whose MCP binary and `.mcpb` bundle were installed and registered during this closeout.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — Restore-closeout session (18:04): handoff note committed and pushed, Zotero MCP binary registered user-scope, backup baseline completed clean, PR monitors left stopped, compiler backlog and parked items carried forward.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/keystone-os-tezino-project.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"KeystoneOS Tezino Narrative Project\"\naliases: [Tezino, Phase 1 data gaps, force plate backfill, CMJ backfill, IMTP, VALD]\ntags: [keystoneos, sports-science, vald, catapult, data-pipeline, force-plate]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-10.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-25.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-10\nupdated: 2026-06-16\n---\n\n# KeystoneOS Tezino Narrative Project\n\nThe Tezino narrative project is a KeystoneOS effort to build a data-driven narrative of an athlete's (Tezino's) physical readiness across a training cycle. As of 2026-06-10 it is in \"Phase 1\": closing data gaps across force plate (CMJ/IMTP), DEXA, and Catapult sources for summer 2025, before moving to \"Phase 2\" — building aligned, days-to-camp datasets. On 2026-06-09, a downstream project (`Tezino_CampPrep`) was initiated, using the injury timeline and force-plate history documented in this narrative project as the empirical foundation for an SPC-based live injury monitoring dashboard for Tezino's 2026 summer camp ramp.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- A summer 2025 CMJ backfill (2025-04-01 to 2025-09-10) pulled 2,531 tests / 103,769 metric rows with zero errors and classified 1,108 of them, bringing team-wide coverage to roughly 105 athletes/month through summer 2025; Tezino specifically gained 11 CMJ tests (Apr-Jul 2025), producing refreshed extracts: `tezino_forceplate_raw.csv` (3,264 rows), `tezino_forceplate_classified.csv` (81 tests), and a new `tezino_imtp.csv` (188 rows).\n- Root cause of the gap: the VALD sync pages `/tests` by `modifiedFromUtc`; daily syncs only started 2026-01-01 and earlier May 2025 backfills stopped at 2024, leaving a seam in summer 2025 coverage — this was a sync-scheduling artifact, not a pipeline bug.\n- Tezino's 2025 summer trend is the inverse of 2024: mRSI dropped from 0.577 (Apr) to 0.518 (Jun) to 0.458 (Jul) to 0.407 (\"white tier\") on the final test of 7/14/2025 — the worst on record, three weeks before camp — while asymmetry stayed near zero, indicating an output decline rather than a movement-quality regression. This aligns with an IMTP slide from 35-38 N/kg to 23.9 N/kg going into camp, and a 284-lb body-weight nadir.\n- Two real testing gaps (not pipeline issues) were identified: no CMJ test in August 2025 despite team-wide testing that month, and no tests at all from 7/14/2025 through April 2026.\n- DEXA was dropped from project scope — the data lives elsewhere and is inaccessible, and the available `.mdb` export was demographics-only anyway.\n- A Catapult sync attempt failed: the configured OpenField token (minted 2026-05-13) was stale/revoked and rejected as \"Unauthenticated\" across all hosts and regions, despite appearing otherwise valid.\n- **Downstream CampPrep project initiated (2026-06-09):** the injury timeline documented in this narrative project — specifically the 5th MT fracture (2024-07-10, acceleration mechanism, S&C) and the parallel hip strain (July 2025, same mechanism, same window) — became the empirical foundation for `Tezino_CampPrep`, an SPC-based live monitoring project for Tezino's 2026 summer ramp. The mRSI decline and IMTP slide documented in Phase 1 contextualize the injury pattern and inform the baseline and warning-band design for the CampPrep XmR charts.\n\n## Details\n\nThe Phase 1 backfill substantially improved data completeness for both the broader team (~105 athletes/month through summer 2025) and Tezino specifically (11 additional CMJ tests plus a brand-new IMTP extract). The findings from this backfill are narratively significant: Tezino's force-output decline through summer 2025, culminating in the worst mRSI on record three weeks before camp, paired with near-zero asymmetry and a matching IMTP and body-weight decline, paints a coherent picture of declining readiness heading into camp rather than an injury-driven movement-quality issue. The database was backed up before the backfill per project convention, and the resulting changes (`docs/DATA-INVENTORY.md`, `docs/NARRATIVE-PLAN.md`, extract script updates) remain uncommitted pending user approval.\n\nTwo anchor dates are needed to align Phase 2's days-to-camp datasets: FC25's camp date is provisionally ~7/30/2025, inferred from the weigh-in cadence flipping from weekly to daily on that date; FC24 has no equivalent data signal and must come from the user, with a provisional fallback of ~7/31/2024. The Catapult sync failure is a separate blocker on the Catapult portion of Phase 1 — the immediate next step is generating a genuinely fresh OpenField token (via Settings → API), re-running `auth set-token`, and verifying both the JWT issue date and `auth status` before re-attempting `sync`. This \"token looks fine but isn't\" failure mode is the mirror image of a credential surprise hit six weeks earlier (2026-04-25) in a different Baylor integration (Perch), where a token that *looked* broken turned out to work fine once the right auth scheme was used — see [[connections/api-credential-assumptions-can-be-backwards]].\n\nThe relationship between the narrative project and the downstream `Tezino_CampPrep` monitoring project is one of empirical foundation → live application. The narrative project's historical data analysis (Phase 1 CMJ/IMTP backfill, injury timeline documentation) answers the question \"what happened to Tezino before each injury?\" The CampPrep project uses those historical findings to design prospective monitoring: the mRSI decline from 0.577 to 0.407 through summer 2025 defines what \"the process that preceded injury\" looked like, and the XmR warning bands in CampPrep are designed to flag a trajectory toward that state before it is reached in 2026. The two projects are complementary: one is retrospective narrative, the other is prospective monitoring, and both depend on the same VALD ForceDecks and Catapult data sources that Phase 1 worked to make complete.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/catapult-connect-api-reference]] — The Catapult token failure here is explained by the OAuth-vs-integration-token behavior documented in the API reference compiled the same day.\n- [[connections/catapult-api-docs-and-tezino-token-issue]] — Explores this same-day connection in depth.\n- [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-deployment]] — An earlier (2026-04-14) Baylor Athletics project whose GPS Sync panel also depends on Catapult API access; see [[connections/catapult-token-friction-across-baylor-projects]] for the recurring pattern of Catapult token issues.\n- [[concepts/skylar-zinnecker-cmj-rtc-reports]] — Another Baylor Athletics project (2026-04-14) built on the same VALD ForceDecks CMJ data source; see [[connections/vald-forcedecks-cmj-data-across-projects]].\n- [[connections/catapult-token-friction-across-baylor-projects]] — Places this project's Catapult token failure in a broader pattern across Baylor projects.\n- [[connections/vald-forcedecks-cmj-data-across-projects]] — Connects this project's CMJ backfill to another Baylor CMJ reporting effort.\n- [[connections/api-credential-assumptions-can-be-backwards]] — Pairs this project's Catapult token failure with a mirror-image Perch auth-scheme surprise from a different Baylor project.\n- [[concepts/tezino-camp-prep-spc-monitoring]] — The downstream SPC-based live monitoring project that uses this narrative project's injury timeline and force-plate history as its empirical foundation for XmR chart design and warning-band placement.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-10.md]] — Phase 1 backfill results, mRSI/IMTP findings, DEXA scope decision, Catapult token failure, and outstanding action items.\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] — Source of the cross-referenced Baylor Load Simulator and Skylar Zinnecker projects linked above.\n- [[daily/2026-04-25.md]] — Source of the mirror-image Perch auth-scheme discovery referenced in [[connections/api-credential-assumptions-can-be-backwards]].\n- [[daily/2026-06-09.md]] — Tezino_CampPrep project initiation (18:03): injury timeline from the narrative project used as empirical foundation for SPC monitoring design; mRSI decline and IMTP data contextualize the CampPrep warning-band design.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/claude-code-token-dashboard.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Claude Code Token Dashboard (nateherkai/token-dashboard)\"\naliases: [token-dashboard, token spend analytics, token-dashboard.bat, token-dashboard.vbs, token-dashboard.db]\ntags: [claude-code, tooling, analytics, windows, infrastructure, monitoring]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-05-21.md\"\ncreated: 2026-05-21\nupdated: 2026-06-15\n---\n\n# Claude Code Token Dashboard (nateherkai/token-dashboard)\n\nOn 2026-05-21, `nateherkai/token-dashboard` was installed at `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\token-dashboard` to provide local, zero-cost analytics on Claude Code token spend. The dashboard is a pure Python HTTP server with no external dependencies, reading JSONL conversation transcripts from `~/.claude/projects/` and serving a real-time analytics UI at `http://127.0.0.1:8080`. A `.bat` launcher was added to the Desktop for one-click access, and a `.vbs` wrapper was placed in `shell:startup` for silent auto-start on login.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Zero dependencies, zero API cost:** the dashboard reads existing JSONL files from `~/.claude/projects/` and processes them locally — it makes no API calls and incurs no Claude token spend. SQLite cache is stored at `~/.claude/token-dashboard.db`.\n- **Network binding:** the server binds to `127.0.0.1` (loopback) only — there is no exposure to other machines even on the same LAN. The server re-scans source files every 30 seconds automatically.\n- **Windows startup automation:** `token-dashboard.bat` on the Desktop provides one-click launch; `token-dashboard.vbs` in `shell:startup` (opened via `Win + R → shell:startup`) runs the dashboard silently at login. A `.vbs` wrapper is preferred over a `.bat` in the Startup folder because `.bat` files open a console window while `.vbs` files invoke `wscript.exe` silently without one.\n- **Claude Code auto-mode trust boundaries:** the auto-mode classifier blocked both (a) execution of freshly-cloned external repo code and (b) writes to the Windows Startup folder, treating both as separate trust thresholds requiring explicit user confirmation. Even after a user confirms the first gate, Startup-folder writes may remain blocked as a hard second gate; the escalation path is manual file creation by the user or adding a `Write` permission rule in `.claude/settings.json`.\n- **Background task management:** when the dashboard server runs as a Claude Code background task (task ID `bolrksfho` in the installation session), it should be stopped via `TaskStop` before ending the session to avoid orphaned server processes.\n\n## Details\n\nThe token dashboard addresses a practical gap in Claude Code's built-in reporting: JSONL transcripts are stored locally and contain full token-count metadata, but there is no native UI to aggregate and visualize cumulative spend across sessions and projects. `nateherkai/token-dashboard` fills this gap with a lightweight local server — no cloud service, no authentication, no ongoing cost — making it well-suited for always-on monitoring via autostart.\n\nThe auto-mode classifier behavior surfaced during setup reveals two distinct trust boundaries Claude Code enforces in autonomous mode. First, executing code from a freshly-cloned external repository (rather than the user's own codebase) triggers a confirmation gate on the grounds that untrusted code should not run without explicit approval. Second, writing to Windows Startup locations (`shell:startup`) is treated as a persistent-access escalation — planting a file that runs on every login is a meaningful trust threshold even if the file's content is benign. These gates apply independently: a user confirming the first does not automatically grant the second. This two-gate behavior is worth understanding because it can feel like a single confirmation \"didn't take\" when in reality it resolved the first gate while the second gate remained. The practical workaround for the Startup write, if the permission rule path is too broad, is to have the user manually place the `.vbs` file themselves in `shell:startup`, entirely outside Claude Code's action surface.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — The broader Claude Code environment audit discipline; the auto-mode trust boundaries encountered during this install represent another class of environment-specific behavior to account for when adding tooling.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-meta-skills-bundle]] — A prior (2026-05-20) session that also dealt with selective Claude Code tooling additions and the trust/relevance evaluation for each addition; shares the \"curate carefully, understand trust implications\" posture.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-05-21.md]] — Installation session (16:35): token dashboard setup, .vbs autostart configuration, auto-mode classifier trust boundary behavior, background task note.\n\n```\n\n### connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Background Task Exit-Code Failures Can Be False Positives\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/speedprofiling-velocity-project\"\n  - \"concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-07-18.md\"\ncreated: 2026-07-18\nupdated: 2026-07-18\n---\n\n# Connection: Background Task Exit-Code Failures Can Be False Positives\n\n## The Connection\n\nOn 2026-07-18, the SpeedProfiling ticket-04 full-game data pull ran as a background R task that reported \"failed / exit code 1,\" yet the task's own log showed a clean end-to-end run — the 10 Hz cache hit, 188 play-by-play plays saved, and the expected outputs written to disk (see [[concepts/speedprofiling-velocity-project]]). This is a specific instance of a recurring class of tooling failure documented across the environment: a status/exit signal reporting failure that does not reflect the actual outcome, where the ground truth lives in a lower-level artifact (a log file, an on-disk output) rather than the reported status code.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nExit codes and background-task status notifications are intermediate signals, not ground truth. A non-zero exit code from a detached or backgrounded process can be an artifact of the process-management layer (shell wrapper, harness notification, cleanup step) rather than a genuine failure of the work the process performed. The correct diagnostic response is to inspect the actual output — the log's final lines, the files on disk, the row counts — before treating the reported failure as real. On 2026-07-18 the SpeedProfiling pull's on-disk verification (8,932,931 rows for all 61/61 athletes, 69/69 tests passing, both caches confirmed hit on re-run) established the run had actually succeeded despite the exit-1 notification.\n\nThis mirrors the memsearch silent `exit 1` failure class (2026-06-13), where a memsearch invocation exited 1 with no surviving diagnostic output — the inverse problem, where a real failure left no trace and could not be diagnosed from the status alone. Together the two cases bracket the same lesson: the exit code and the actual outcome are independent signals that must be reconciled by examining the primitive artifacts. This is the process-execution analog of the broader \"verify directly, don't trust the intermediate layer\" discipline: just as agent citations, preview-server renders, and retrieval scores can misrepresent ground truth, so can a process's reported exit status.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-07-18 (14:09): \"background task reported exit 1 but the log showed a clean end-to-end run (cache hit on 10 Hz data, 188 pbp plays saved)\"; separately logged as a lesson — \"Background task notification showed 'failed / exit code 1' even though the R script completed cleanly — check the log before assuming failure.\"\n- On-disk verification confirmed success: 8,932,931 rows at true 10 Hz for all 61/61 athletes, 188 pbp plays, 69/69 testthat tests pass, both caches hit on re-run.\n- 2026-06-13: memsearch produced a silent `exit 1` \"with no surviving diagnostic output\" — the complementary case where the exit code was the only signal and no artifact survived to reconcile it against (see [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]]).\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/speedprofiling-velocity-project]] — The project where the ticket-04 background pull produced the false exit-1 notification.\n- [[concepts/memsearch-tool-maintenance]] — Documents the complementary memsearch silent exit-1 failure class.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — The broader discipline of verifying against ground-truth artifacts rather than trusting an intermediate layer; the exit-code false failure is the process-execution instance of that pattern.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-08-07.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Outlook Inbox Triage Action List (2026-08-07 Run)\"\naliases: [2026-08-07 inbox triage, needs-reply 8-07, SOC suspicious PowerShell 4 days, Courteney Tompkins slideshow, Cody Soto Applied Performance website, Angus Walsh VALD integration, Jason Riddell season prep checklist, Paul Goncharow Kactis, RJ Archer ShiftWave 17 days stale, Robbie De La Pena sports analytics]\ntags: [productivity, workflow, email, communication, triage, baylor-athletics, inbox, security]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-07.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-07\nupdated: 2026-08-14\n---\n\n# Outlook Inbox Triage Action List (2026-08-07 Run)\n\nOn 2026-08-07, a morning Outlook inbox triage produced a structured JSON backlog of pending replies, follow-ups, meetings to schedule, and forms owed for the Baylor Applied Performance role. The run is another instance of the recurring [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]], and it is a direct day-after continuation of the [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-08-06]] run: the same SOC \"Suspicious PowerShell command line\" security alert again ranked as the single highest-urgency item, now aged from 3 to 4 days outstanding.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Highest-urgency item is again a security alert:** the SOC \"Suspicious PowerShell command line\" incident, now 4 days outstanding, was flagged for immediate investigation/response — a security-sensitive event again outranking older relational replies under the age × importance model (see [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]]).\n- **Seven needs-reply threads:** SOC PowerShell alert (4d), Courteney Tompkins re: Fall Student Athlete Slideshow (today), Angus Walsh re: VALD | Baylor integration (2d), Jason Riddell re: season prep checklist (1d), Cody Soto re: Applied Performance Website (2d), Paul Goncharow re: Kactis (3d), Ramsey Sharayha re: student worker hiring/onboarding (3d).\n- **Stale follow-up:** RJ Archer / ShiftWave, last contacted 2026-07-21 and now 17 days stale, flagged for a re-ping.\n- **Meetings to schedule:** Dayannara Munoz (Kinexon implementation planning), Robbie De La Pena (Sports Analytics help, Mon–Thu), Sayawni Lassiter (Complete Sports CEO call — prior 7/24 slot stale, needs a new time).\n- **Immediate forms/actions owed:** sign the Baylor/VALD AI Beta consent form (DocuSign); confirm the Applied Performance email subscription. A 15:02 memory flush returned FLUSH_OK — the triage was the only durable output of the day.\n\n## Details\n\nThe 2026-08-07 triage reinforces the age × importance prioritization that governs the recurring methodology: the SOC suspicious-PowerShell alert, though not the oldest item, was elevated above every relational and staffing reply because a potential security event carries a severity that a stalled reply does not. This is the second consecutive day the same SOC alert has topped the ranking — on 2026-08-06 it was 3 days old and highest-urgency, and on 2026-08-07 it is 4 days old and still unresolved, making the day-over-day increment of an unactioned security item visible in the same way overdue timecards surfaced in earlier runs. The pattern echoes the multi-day overdue counters tracked in [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-06-04]] and its neighbours, where an obligation's age was allowed to increment across consecutive digests without the digest itself forcing resolution.\n\nMany correspondents recur directly from the prior day, confirming a near-identical queue aging forward one day: the SOC alert, Cody Soto (Applied Performance website), Angus Walsh (VALD | Baylor integration), Ramsey Sharayha (student worker onboarding), Dayannara Munoz (Kinexon implementation planning), Sayawni Lassiter (Complete Sports CEO call, still a stale 7/24 slot), the VALD AI Beta consent DocuSign, and the Applied Performance email-subscription confirmation all carry across from [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-08-06]]. New this run are Courteney Tompkins (same-day Fall Student Athlete Slideshow), Jason Riddell (season prep checklist), Paul Goncharow (Kactis), and Robbie De La Pena (Sports Analytics help, available Mon–Thu). The RJ Archer / ShiftWave follow-up, last touched 2026-07-21, has now reached 17 days silent, exemplifying the stalled-follow-up subcategory where a re-ping rather than an initial reply is owed; the same thread appears at 15 days on 2026-08-06 and at 3 days on the 2026-07-24 run, giving a three-point trace of the same unanswered outreach. As with prior runs, the triage established a prioritized ground truth without executing replies; batched action follows the digest.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/inbox-triage-methodology]] — The structured 6-category audit this run instantiates; the needs-reply / follow-ups / meetings-to-schedule / forms-owed bucketing and age × importance prioritization are defined there.\n- [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-08-06]] — The immediate prior-day run; the SOC PowerShell alert and most needs-reply/forms threads carry forward one day, with the alert aging from 3 to 4 days outstanding.\n- [[concepts/outlook-inbox-triage-2026-07-24]] — An earlier run in the same series where the RJ Archer / ShiftWave and Sayawni Lassiter threads first appear; comparing the three shows those threads aging across two-plus weeks.\n- [[concepts/kinexon-volleyball-tracking-project]] — The Dayannara Munoz Kinexon implementation-planning meeting connects to the Baylor volleyball Kinexon tracking workstream.\n- [[concepts/morning-email-briefing-design]] — The automated briefing system whose 6-section taxonomy this manual digest mirrors.\n- [[connections/unactioned-urgent-items-age-across-digests]] — The cross-cutting pattern this run instantiates: a digest surfaces urgency but does not resolve it, so the same top-ranked item recurs with an incremented counter.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-07.md]] — Inbox triage session (11:06): structured JSON backlog of 7 needs-reply threads (SOC PowerShell alert 4d highest-urgency, Courteney Tompkins same-day slideshow, Angus Walsh VALD, Jason Riddell season prep, Cody Soto website, Paul Goncharow Kactis, Ramsey Sharayha student worker), RJ Archer/ShiftWave 17 days stale, meetings to schedule (Dayannara Munoz, Robbie De La Pena Mon–Thu, Sayawni Lassiter), forms owed (VALD AI Beta consent DocuSign, Applied Performance email subscription); 15:02 memory flush FLUSH_OK.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Perch/Weight Room Domain Feature\"\naliases: [Perch, Weight Room domain, perch.duckdb, perch_ingest.py, report.html.j2, trend-wr chart]\ntags: [software-development, subagent-driven-development, jinja2, reporting, weight-room, baylor-athletics, perch]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-24.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-25.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-24\nupdated: 2026-06-15\n---\n\n# Perch/Weight Room Domain Feature\n\nOn 2026-04-24, the final task (Task 9: HTML template) of a 9-task \"Perch/Weight Room domain\" feature was completed using [[concepts/subagent-driven-development]], closing out a full implementation that included updated `CLAUDE.md` and `CONTEXT.md` documentation and a final round of commits to Git. The following day (2026-04-25), the feature's last remaining action item - populating `data/perch.duckdb` via the Perch API - was completed, fully activating the Weight Room domain in production reports.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Task 9 (the HTML report template, `report.html.j2`) was the last of nine tasks defined in the plan at `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-04-24-perch-weight-room-domain.md`.\n- A code-quality reviewer caught a real bug during Task 9: two chart-cleanup arrays in `report.html.j2` were missing a `\"trend-wr\"` (Weight Room trend) entry; the implementer fixed it and the code was re-reviewed successfully before merge.\n- Trend chart data in `report.html.j2` uses the [[concepts/jinja2-none-safety-pattern]] (`{% if value is not none %}`) rather than Jinja2's `default()` filter, for reliable handling of optional/missing metric values.\n- The spec-compliance reviewer passed Task 9 12/12 on its compliance checklist before the separate code-quality review caught the `trend-wr` bug - illustrating the two-stage review process described in [[concepts/subagent-driven-development]].\n- On 2026-04-25, [[concepts/perch-api-integration-reference]] documents how the data-activation step was completed: `src/perch_ingest.py` was debugged and rewritten against the real Perch API, then run to populate `data/perch.duckdb` with 436 athletes and 12,300 set records, after which the Weight Room domain activates automatically on the next report run.\n\n## Details\n\nThe 2026-04-24 session was a wrap-up session for a feature that had been built incrementally across nine planned tasks. By this point, eight tasks were already complete, and the final task - producing the HTML report template that renders Weight Room scores and trend charts - was handed to [[concepts/subagent-driven-development]] for implementation and review. The naming (\"Weight Room domain,\" trend charts including a `\"trend-wr\"` series) suggests this feature models strength/weight-room training data for reporting purposes, consistent with the broader sports-science and athletic-performance tooling that recurs elsewhere in this knowledge base. No explicit organization was named in the 2026-04-24 log, but the following day's session confirms the organization is Baylor (Perch org ID 959, Baylor University), tying this feature into the broader Baylor Athletics tooling ecosystem alongside [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-deployment]] and [[concepts/keystone-os-tezino-project]].\n\nCompletion of Task 9 was not purely mechanical: the code-quality review stage caught an omission (missing `\"trend-wr\"` entries in two separate chart-cleanup arrays) that had passed both the implementer's own work and the spec-compliance reviewer's 12/12 pass. This is a concrete example of why a second, differently-focused review pass matters - see [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] for the broader pattern. With all nine tasks done and 26/26 tests passing across 11 commits, the feature itself was code-complete on 2026-04-24; what remained was the data-activation step - running `perch_ingest.py` against `data/perch.duckdb`, after first running `--probe` to verify the ingest script's API field-name constants. That probe step, run on 2026-04-25, turned out to be essential: nearly every assumption embedded in the original `perch_ingest.py` (auth scheme, primary data endpoint, user-list endpoint and response shape, pagination model) was wrong, and [[concepts/perch-api-integration-reference]] documents the corrected values. Once corrected, the full ingest succeeded, `CLAUDE.md`/`CONTEXT.md` were updated again, and the change was pushed to `main` as `5cdb51e` - completing the feature end-to-end, from implementation through data activation, across two consecutive days.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/subagent-driven-development]] - The methodology used to implement and review Task 9, including the two-stage review that caught the `trend-wr` bug.\n- [[concepts/jinja2-none-safety-pattern]] - The None-handling pattern applied to trend chart data in `report.html.j2` during Task 9.\n- [[concepts/perch-api-integration-reference]] - The 2026-04-25 follow-up session that completed this feature's data-activation step by fixing the Perch API integration.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-24.md]] - Task 9 completion and full 9-task feature wrap-up session (17:06).\n- [[daily/2026-04-25.md]] - Data-activation session (16:06): Perch API debugging/rewrite and full ingest run.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/keystoneos-repo-intake-flow.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"KeystoneOS Repo Intake Flow (inventory → intake → promote)\"\naliases: [KeystoneOS intake, repo ingestion flow, inventory intake promote, cmj_traffic_light, PlayerProfiles_FB, OneDrive shadow repos, DEV canonical path, active candidate promotion, project-intake report, review bucket]\ntags: [keystoneos, project-management, workflow, tooling, powershell, automation, git]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-07-13.md\"\ncreated: 2026-07-13\nupdated: 2026-08-01\n---\n\n# KeystoneOS Repo Intake Flow (inventory → intake → promote)\n\nThe KeystoneOS repo intake flow is the AGENTS.md-defined three-stage pipeline — `inventory` → `intake` → `promote` — for discovering repositories across configured discovery roots and registering genuinely new ones in the KeystoneOS project registry. On 2026-07-13, a run scanned 58 candidates (14 already registered), promoted two genuinely new repos as \"Active candidate,\" left 17 low-signal items in the review bucket, and committed the registry update under standing auto-commit authorization.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Three-stage flow:** `inventory` scans discovery roots for candidate repos; `intake` scores and buckets candidates, writing a report (`outputs/intake/2026-07-08-project-intake.md`); `promote` registers selected candidates in the registry. Each item in the intake report carries its own recommended promote command.\n- **2026-07-13 results:** 58 candidates scanned, 14 already registered; two new repos promoted — `cmj_traffic_light` (CMJ recovery monitoring for Volleyball, 4 commits, registered at its OneDrive work-drive path as its only home) and `PlayerProfiles_FB_03_28_2026` (football player profiles with Perch API ingest, 43 commits, registered at canonical `DEV\\` path). 17 low-signal items left unpromoted in the review bucket.\n- **DEV is canonical; skip OneDrive shadows:** OneDrive INBOX copies of Travel_Map, ForcePlate_DecisionSystem, and PlayerProfiles were skipped as shadows of already-registered DEV repos, per drive-topology policy. A repo whose only home is OneDrive (cmj_traffic_light) is registered at that path; a repo with a canonical DEV copy is registered there and its OneDrive duplicate ignored.\n- **Ambiguous scope flagged, not assumed:** the user's \"new repo ingestion\" phrasing was interpreted as discover-and-register-all-new, but the assistant flagged the assumption and offered to promote specific review-bucket repos instead if that was the intent.\n- **JSON schema and PowerShell gotchas:** the intake report's JSON schemas differed from initial assumptions — inspect shapes before parsing and use the report's own recommended promote commands. PowerShell `Select-Object` clipped formatted `keystone.ps1 health` output; rerun `health` plain rather than piping through `Select-Object`.\n\n## Details\n\nThe intake flow operationalizes the earlier DEV-sweep classification model (see [[concepts/keystoneos-project-registry]]) as a repeatable, command-driven pipeline. Where the DEV sweep bucketed projects into confirmed-live / ambiguous / inactive, the intake flow produces a scored candidate report and registers only genuinely new, sufficiently-signaled repos as \"Active candidate,\" leaving low-signal items in a review bucket for explicit user decision. The conservatism is the same: ambiguous or low-signal repos are never auto-promoted, and the run flagged its own interpretation of an ambiguous user instruction rather than proceeding silently. The two promotions on 2026-07-13 illustrate the drive-topology policy in action — `cmj_traffic_light` was registered at its OneDrive path because that is its only home, while `PlayerProfiles_FB_03_28_2026` was registered at its canonical `DEV\\` path and its OneDrive INBOX duplicate skipped, consistent with the rule that DEV is canonical and OneDrive copies are shadows.\n\nTwo operational gotchas surfaced. First, the intake report's JSON output did not match the shapes initially assumed for parsing; the correct practice is to inspect the actual JSON structure before writing parse logic and to use each item's own recommended promote command rather than reconstructing commands independently. Second, piping the formatted output of `keystone.ps1 health` through PowerShell's `Select-Object` clipped the display — a rendering artifact of PowerShell's formatted-object pipeline rather than a data problem — so the reliable path is to rerun `health` plain without the pipe. The registry update was committed and pushed to main (`7140ad1`) under the KeystoneOS repo's standing auto-commit authorization, and review-bucket candidates (e.g., AT_Utilization_Benchmarks, baylor-gps-football) remain unpromoted pending user direction.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/keystoneos-project-registry]] — The earlier DEV-sweep registry model this intake flow implements as a command-driven pipeline; both share the three-bucket classification discipline and the \"never auto-promote ambiguous candidates\" rule.\n- [[concepts/model-agnostic-repo-dual-context-files]] — The AGENTS.md routing table that the project registry feeds; the intake flow is itself defined by AGENTS.md, making AGENTS.md both the routing surface and the process specification.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — Inspecting the intake report's actual JSON shapes before parsing (rather than assuming) is an instance of verifying the ground-truth structure rather than trusting an assumed intermediate format.\n- [[concepts/perch-api-integration-reference]] — `PlayerProfiles_FB` uses the Perch API ingest documented there; the same credentials are reused across Perch-dependent Baylor projects.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-07-13.md]] — Repo intake session (16:12): 58 candidates scanned, `cmj_traffic_light` and `PlayerProfiles_FB_03_28_2026` promoted, OneDrive shadows skipped per drive-topology policy, ambiguous-scope assumption flagged, JSON-shape and `Select-Object` gotchas documented, committed `7140ad1` under auto-commit authorization.\n\n```\n\n### connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import\"\n  - \"concepts/the-monitor-newsletter\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-22.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-22\nupdated: 2026-04-22\n---\n\n# Connection: Verify Directly, Don't Trust the Intermediate Layer\n\n## The Connection\n\nBoth substantive sessions on 2026-04-22 ran into a version of the same problem: a tool or agent produced output that *looked* fine - a citation with a plausible author list, a photo wired into HTML with a correct relative path - but was actually wrong or unverifiable through the usual checking method. In [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]], a background repair agent's citation fixes for IDs 286, 287, and 358 contained hallucinated author lists despite correctly identifying the papers' topics. In [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]], Blake Wright's photo failed to appear in the localhost preview server even though the HTML and file path were both correct.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nIn both cases, the fix was not to redo the original work but to change the *verification method* to something closer to the ground truth. The hallucinated citations were caught by extracting the actual PDF text with PyPDF2 and reading it directly, rather than trusting the agent's summarized output. The \"broken\" photo was confirmed to be fine by opening the HTML file directly in Chrome via `file://`, rather than trusting the brainstorm preview server's rendering. The shared lesson: when an agent, tool, or service produces output that looks plausible but can't be easily double-checked through the same channel, step down to a more primitive, lower-level check (raw file text, direct file rendering) before accepting the result as correct.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 2026-04-22 (10:57): \"Background agent produced `docs/batch_r7_fixes.json` but hallucinated wrong author lists for IDs 286, 287, and 358... Used PyPDF2 text extraction to verify actual authors and corrected all three citations before committing.\"\n- 2026-04-22 (17:21): \"Photo failed to render in localhost preview — identified as a server limitation (brainstorm preview server doesn't serve image files), not an HTML error... always verify photo rendering by opening the HTML directly in Chrome via file:// URL.\"\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]]\n- [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]]\n\n```\n\n### concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"CRASHBOX SSD Backup Baseline and Mid-Run Volume Rename\"\naliases: [CRASHBOX, SSD backup baseline, AssistantBackup, backup manifest path, volume rename mid-backup, rsync incremental resume, diskutil rename, local-only backup, Drive upload deferral]\ntags: [backup, macos, storage, automation, rsync, workflow, machine-restore]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-11\nupdated: 2026-08-11\n---\n\n# CRASHBOX SSD Backup Baseline and Mid-Run Volume Rename\n\nOn 2026-08-11, as part of a machine-restore checklist closeout, an external SSD was renamed to `CRASHBOX` and used to capture the first full local backup baseline at `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup`. The rename occurred while a backup run was already in flight; the run was restarted against the new path and completed clean (OK, 0 errors) at 21:25. The session also established which parts of the backup pipeline require network connectivity and which do not.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **First full CRASHBOX baseline completed clean:** OK with 0 errors at 21:25, after the backup manifest was repointed to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup` and the in-flight run restarted.\n- **Mid-backup volume rename is safe.** Already-copied data survives the rename, and rsync's incremental behavior means a restarted run — or one interrupted by power loss — resumes rather than redoing completed work.\n- **Volume names containing spaces work fine in the PowerShell backup manifest;** the rename to `CRASHBOX` was cosmetic, not a correctness fix.\n- **Network dependency is confined to one step.** The SSD copy is fully local; Google Drive writes cache locally and upload later; only the git commit/push step needs the network, and it self-heals on the next nightly run. The machine could therefore be safely disconnected and shut down mid-pipeline.\n- **The rename was performed via a one-off manual-mode approval** rather than a standing `Bash(diskutil rename:*)` allow rule, since no recurrence was expected.\n\n## Details\n\nThe backup pipeline has three write targets with different durability and connectivity characteristics, and the session made this stratification explicit. The local SSD copy is synchronous and offline-capable. The Google Drive leg writes to a locally-cached CloudStorage path and defers the actual upload, so it neither blocks nor requires connectivity at run time — the same local-cache-then-upload behavior that previously caused a receipt read-back race when a job read a freshly-written receipt before Drive had flushed it. Only the git commit and push step genuinely needs the network, and because the backup job runs nightly, a failed push is self-healing: the next run retries it. This meant the correct answer to \"do I need internet before shutting down?\" was no, with a single deferred and self-recovering exception.\n\nThe mid-run rename was the session's operationally interesting move. Renaming a mounted volume changes the `/Volumes/<name>` mount path, which invalidates any manifest entry or in-progress run referencing the old path — but it does not touch the copied files themselves. Because the backup uses rsync semantics, restarting against the corrected path performs an incremental pass over the already-transferred data rather than a full recopy, so the cost of the restart is proportional to what remained rather than to the total. This same property makes the run resilient to power interruption, which matters for a first full baseline that transfers the largest volume of data it ever will. The rename itself was cosmetic; the manifest tolerated the prior name, spaces included.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/launchd-backup-receipt-race-repo-reconciliation]] — The same backup subsystem, where a Google Drive CloudStorage write-lag raced a receipt read-back and the job was moved under `launchd`; this article records the local-SSD leg and its offline-capable characteristics.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-permission-modes]] — The rename was gated by the permission system and resolved via a one-off manual-mode approval rather than a standing allow rule.\n- [[concepts/automation-stack-doctor]] — The broader scheduled-automation stack (nightly backup task, reindex, cloud checks) that this baseline run belongs to.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — Explains why durable, long-running backup work belongs in an OS-scheduled job rather than an in-session background command.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-11.md]] — Restore-closeout session (18:04): SSD renamed to `CRASHBOX`, manifest repointed to `/Volumes/CRASHBOX/AssistantBackup`, in-flight run restarted, network-dependency analysis (local SSD / deferred Drive upload / self-healing git push), first full baseline completed OK with 0 errors at 21:25.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/background-monitor-signal-reliability.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Background Monitor Signal Reliability (Dropped, Duplicated, and Noisy Events)\"\naliases: [monitor dropped events, session-limit lockout drops events, monitor dedup backoff, Greptile comment-count noise, PR monitor false CI failure, monitor task reliability]\ntags: [claude-code, automation, monitoring, github, ci, reliability, gotcha, workflow]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-06.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-06\nupdated: 2026-08-06\n---\n\n# Background Monitor Signal Reliability (Dropped, Duplicated, and Noisy Events)\n\nBackground monitor tasks that watch GitHub PRs for CI status and code-review activity emit signals that cannot be trusted at face value. On 2026-08-06, two distinct failure modes surfaced in a single day: monitor events fired during a session-usage-limit lockout were silently dropped (the same CI-failure event re-fired ~15 times with no dedup and no user alert), and Greptile PR comment-count increments proved noisy because the monitor counts the user's own replies as if they were new findings. Both reinforce that a monitor event is a hint to verify ground truth, not an actionable fact.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Events during a session-limit lockout are silently dropped:** monitor task `bum0ne2se` fired ~15 repeated \"PR#1667: CI FAILURE detected\" events, but every one arrived while the session was at its usage limit (resets 12:30 PM CT), so none produced triage or a `PushNotification`. The identical event re-fired repeatedly with no deduplication and no user alert.\n- **No dedup / no backoff amplifies the problem:** because the monitor has no dedup or backoff, a single unresolved condition (one CI failure) generates a storm of identical dropped events rather than one, and the lockout makes all of them invisible.\n- **Comment-count events are noisy on review PRs:** Greptile PR comment-count `+1`/`+2` increments are often the user's own replies, not new findings — verify the top-level finding count before acting on a comment-count bump.\n- **Mitigations:** add dedup/backoff to the monitor, and provide an alternate notification path (e.g., `PushNotification`) that does not depend on model availability so lockout-period events still surface.\n- **Runaway monitors must be stopped:** a monitor re-firing duplicate events for the same unresolved failure should be reviewed and stopped (`TaskStop`) once the underlying issue is understood, to prevent it from re-firing indefinitely.\n\n## Details\n\nThe session-limit drop is the more consequential failure because it defeats the monitor's entire purpose: the one time an alert is most needed (a CI failure while the user is away and locked out) is exactly when the delivery path is unavailable. Model-dependent notification means the notification competes for the same usage budget the lockout has already exhausted, so the event evaporates. The architectural lesson is that a monitor's alerting path should be decoupled from model availability — a lightweight push notification or an out-of-band log write can surface the condition without a model turn. Layering dedup and exponential backoff on top ensures that an unresolved condition produces one durable alert rather than a repeating storm, and that the storm does not itself consume the budget that would deliver the alert.\n\nThe comment-count noise is a subtler trust problem. A rising comment count on a Greptile review PR looks like new findings, but during an iterative remediation loop the user's own reply comments inflate the same counter. Acting on the raw count leads to re-investigating threads that are already answered. The correct primitive to check is the top-level finding count, not the comment count — a distinction between \"activity on the PR\" and \"unresolved findings on the PR.\" Both failure modes are instances of the same discipline seen elsewhere in this knowledge base: a monitor emits an intermediate proxy signal, and the proxy diverges from the ground truth precisely under the conditions that matter, so the signal must be reconciled against the authoritative source (CI run status, finding count) before it drives action.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[connections/background-task-exit-code-false-failure]] — Background task exit-1 notifications can be false positives; the monitor's false/duplicate/dropped CI events are the same class of unreliable background signal requiring reconciliation against on-disk or remote ground truth.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — Comment-count-as-findings and dropped-event-as-no-failure are both cases of an intermediate proxy diverging from ground truth; verify the authoritative source directly.\n- [[concepts/in-session-background-process-limits]] — In-session background work is coupled to the session's lifecycle and budget; the lockout-drop failure is a related coupling problem where the alerting path shares the session's exhausted usage budget.\n- [[concepts/greptile-iterative-review-credential-consistency]] — The PR #1668 Greptile remediation loop where the comment-count noise was observed firsthand.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-06.md]] — Session-limit lockout session (12:30): monitor `bum0ne2se` re-firing ~15 dropped \"CI FAILURE\" events with no dedup/alert during a usage lockout. Loose-ends sweep (18:02): Greptile comment-count `+1`/`+2` increments identified as the user's own replies rather than new findings.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/athlete-monitoring-rtp-presentation.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Athlete Monitoring / Return-to-Play Conference Presentation Design\"\naliases: [RTP presentation, athlete monitoring slide deck, sports science conference slides, z-score traffic light thresholds, stakeholder report slide, frontend-slides sports science, visual-first sports presentation, 11-slide RTP structure, 14-slide RTP deck, single-athlete framing presentation, on-dark CSS slide deck, synthetic keydown browser automation unreliable, You Have the Data Now What, RTP-Data-Collection-SCRIPT, RTP deck v1 v2, slide 8 chart overflow fix, twenty-minute gap analysis]\ntags: [baylor-athletics, sports-science, presentation, communication, return-to-play, athlete-monitoring, workflow, deliverables]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-17.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-18.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-17\nupdated: 2026-08-07\n---\n\n# Athlete Monitoring / Return-to-Play Conference Presentation Design\n\nOn 2026-06-17, a conference presentation slide deck (~20-min talk) was designed for a visually-oriented sports science audience covering athlete monitoring and return-to-play data workflows. The design process resolved four key architectural decisions — framing, statistical thresholds, content constraints, and audience philosophy — and produced a locked 11-slide running order built around a single-athlete RTP narrative with minimal text and strong visual anchors. A subsequent validation pass confirmed all slides were layout-clean and fixed a dark-mode visibility bug on the final slide. On 2026-06-18, a full speaker script was written for the deck (`RTP-Data-Collection-SCRIPT.md`), a duration analysis showed the 11-slide deck ran far short of its 20-minute slot, and the deck was expanded to 14 slides (v2) with v1 preserved.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Single-athlete framing throughout:** roster-comparison views were explicitly rejected as \"off-topic for the individual RTP framing.\" The presentation's decision-support narrative only holds together when the audience follows one athlete's trajectory from injury through return-to-competition decisions. Adding a roster view introduces a different clinical use case that fragments narrative coherence.\n- **Visual-first audience philosophy:** the intended audience \"eats with their eyes\" — conversational delivery carries the explanatory load while slides provide visual anchors, not text. Minimal text per slide; strong imagery and color-coded indicators are the design primitives.\n- **Z-score traffic-light thresholds: −1.5 SD / −2 SD.** Applied in the Excel demo to translate raw metrics into actionable status flags. −1.5 SD = caution/yellow; −2 SD = concern/red. The leave-behind template implements the same logic, ensuring the presentation and the artifact practitioners actually use share the same statistical foundation.\n- **Screenshots-only (no live build):** consistent with the leave-behind template format. All data visualization examples appear as screenshots rather than live-built charts, eliminating technical failure risk in a conference setting.\n- **11-slide running order:** bridge → decision-first framing (×2) → generalizing → metric selection → blank template → populated/flagged → charted → stakeholder report → take-home materials → close.\n- **Slide 9 — Sample Stakeholder Report:** single-athlete trend chart + color-coded status flag + one plain-language interpretive line. Models the exact artifact the audience would produce and deliver to coaching/medical staff, completing the \"here is the workflow\" → \"here is the output\" arc.\n- **Josse referenced verbally only (not on-screen):** a named methodological reference was kept off slides for sensitivity reasons; verbal attribution suffices for academic credentialing without creating a circulating visual artifact.\n- **Slide validation (Session 23:56):** All 11 slides confirmed layout-clean at 1440×900 and 375×667. Synthetic keyboard event dispatch (`keydown`) is unreliable for slide navigation in browser automation — smooth-scroll does not complete on synthetic key events in browser extension automation contexts. Use direct navigation-dot element clicks for reliable automated HTML slide deck validation.\n- **Dark-background slides require adaptive nav chrome:** Slide 11 uses a dark background (`rgb(17,17,17)`); the default nav-dot and slide-counter colors (`rgba(0,0,0,0.45)`) are invisible against it. Fix: an `on-dark` CSS class toggled on `<body>` by the `setActive()` JavaScript function enables CSS overrides for nav elements on dark slides. This pattern generalizes to any HTML slide deck with alternating light/dark slide backgrounds.\n- **A tight, purposeful deck runs far shorter than its slot (2026-06-18):** the 11-slide \"You Have the Data. Now What?\" deck at correct density runs ~5–7 minutes (7–9 with pauses), leaving an 11–14 minute gap against the 20-minute target. Closing the gap requires real content (case-study athlete walkthrough, live-demo narration for the data table and chart slides, planted audience Q&A), not padding — slide count and density are the drivers, not verbosity.\n- **Speaker script + v1/v2 versioning (2026-06-18):** a full per-slide speaker script with talking points, notes, and pacing was saved to `outputs/presentation/RTP-Data-Collection-SCRIPT.md`. The deck was expanded to 14 slides; the original 11-slide version was reconstructed from git and preserved as `RTP-Data-Collection-v1.html`, the 14-slide version saved as `RTP-Data-Collection-v2.html`, with the working file `RTP-Data-Collection.html` aliasing v2. Preserving v1 before editing follows an \"always keep the original\" versioning discipline.\n- **Slide 8 chart overflow fix (2026-06-18):** the slide-8 line chart overflowed at short/landscape viewports (~896×414); fixed with `aspect-ratio` plus `max-height: min(40vh, 320px)`. This is a second responsive-layout defect (alongside the slide-11 dark-theme fix) that only surfaced at non-standard viewport sizes, reinforcing multi-viewport render verification.\n\n## Details\n\nThe presentation's core design challenge is bridging the gap between analytical rigor and practitioner legibility for a mixed audience of sports scientists, athletic trainers, and coaches. A talk that leads with methodology (sensor setup, reliability coefficients, statistical assumptions) loses practitioners in the first two minutes; a talk that shows only colored flags without any basis loses the sports scientists. The \"decision-first framing (×2)\" in the running order addresses this: the talk opens with the end product — the practitioner's decision — and only then traces backward to the analytical workflow that supports it. The repetition reinforces the framing before technical detail begins.\n\nThe z-score threshold design (−1.5 / −2 SD) was chosen for the Excel demo because it is transferable: any clinician working with any monitoring dataset can understand and apply standard deviation cutoffs without statistical training beyond \"how far from the average.\" The traffic-light metaphor (green/yellow/red) maps intuitively to go/caution/stop decisions without requiring the audience to interpret numbers directly. The leave-behind template implements the same −1.5 / −2 SD thresholds automatically, so practitioners who use it after the session are working with the same statistical logic demonstrated in the talk — reducing drift toward arbitrary threshold modification post-session.\n\nSlide 9's stakeholder report design was added specifically to address the \"so what do I hand to the coach/AT?\" question that sports scientists routinely face after building a monitoring pipeline. The three-element structure (trend chart + status flag + one interpretive sentence) is the minimum viable stakeholder report: more complex outputs tend to go unread or generate confusion when shared with coaching staff who do not routinely interpret data visualizations. The time budget for the talk was kept deliberately soft — enriching content depth was prioritized over strict adherence to 20 minutes, on the premise that a substantive overrun is less damaging than a rushed, thin presentation for an engaged sports science audience. The frontend-slides skill was loaded after all content decisions were locked to execute the build phase.\n\nFollowing the content design session (20:28), a validation pass (Session 23:56) confirmed all 11 slides rendered correctly at both standard and mobile viewport sizes. The initial browser automation approach used synthetic keydown events to navigate between slides — this proved unreliable because the presentation's smooth-scroll transition does not complete when triggered by a synthetic key event in browser extension automation contexts. Switching to direct navigation-dot element clicks resolved this. A dark-mode visibility bug was discovered on Slide 11, whose dark background (`rgb(17,17,17)`) made both the slide counter text and the inactive navigation dots invisible in their default dark-gray color (`rgba(0,0,0,0.45)`). The fix uses a `setActive()` JavaScript function that toggles an `on-dark` class on the `<body>` element each time a new slide becomes active.\n\nThe 2026-06-18 work moved the deck from a validated visual artifact toward a delivery-ready talk. Writing the speaker script exposed a duration mismatch that the slide count alone had masked: a deck deliberately built for high visual density and low text runs quickly when narrated, so the 11-slide deck consumed only ~5–7 minutes of a 20-minute slot. The assistant explicitly declined to \"pad\" the existing script and instead framed three substance-based gap-closure paths (expand the script in place, add 4–6 slides, or a hybrid), identifying the data-table and chart slides, a case-study athlete walkthrough, and planted audience-participation moments as the highest-value additions. The deck was then expanded to 14 slides (v2), and the original 11-slide deck was reconstructed from git and preserved as v1 — an instance of keeping the prior version before making a structural edit. A second responsive defect was fixed during this pass: the slide-8 line chart overflowed at short landscape viewports and was constrained with `aspect-ratio` and a `max-height: min(40vh, 320px)` cap, echoing the slide-11 dark-theme fix as a layout bug invisible at the default viewport but present at edge sizes.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/skylar-zinnecker-cmj-rtc-reports]] — Individual RTC reports built from VALD ForceDecks data for a single athlete; shares the single-athlete framing, Baylor institutional branding, and color-coded status indicators (Exceeds Baseline / Progressing) with the stakeholder report slide design.\n- [[concepts/tezino-camp-prep-spc-monitoring]] — SPC-based monitoring dashboard with first-class explainability requirements for coaching and medical staff; shares the \"every metric needs a plain-language justification\" communication principle and threshold-based flag logic.\n- [[concepts/baylor-wtn-acwr-analysis]] — The dual-model (actual vs. compliant counterfactual) ACWR overlay is another athlete monitoring communication artifact designed to make statistical content interpretable for practitioners — the same decision-support framing used in the presentation.\n- [[connections/athlete-monitoring-communication-grammar]] — The cross-cutting visual vocabulary (color coding, threshold flags, trend + flag + sentence) that independently recurs across the presentation, Zinnecker reports, Tezino SPC monitoring, and the Swiss Modern coaching deck.\n- [[concepts/sports-coaching-slide-design-system]] — A parallel S&C coaching staff slide deck built the same day (Session 23:43) using the Swiss Modern preset; shares the traffic-light color constraint and HTML slide build context. The two projects together document complementary implementation gotchas: coaching deck (box-sizing, file:// URL mangling) and RTP presentation (synthetic keydown unreliability, dark-background CSS toggle, slide-8 chart overflow).\n- [[concepts/rtp-clinic-deliverable-finalization]] — The parallel 31-slide Eric+Jeremy clinic deck; both efforts involve speaker-script restructuring, versioned deck files, and multi-viewport render verification, but this is the shorter single-author conference talk.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-17.md]] — Conference presentation design session (20:28): four design decisions locked, 11-slide running order established, Slide 9 stakeholder report format designed. Validation session (23:56): all 11 slides confirmed layout-clean at 1440×900 and 375×667, synthetic keydown unreliable, switched to dot-click navigation, dark-background Slide 11 visibility bug fixed via `on-dark` body class toggle.\n- [[daily/2026-06-18.md]] — Speaker-script and duration session (01:12): full per-slide script saved to `RTP-Data-Collection-SCRIPT.md`, 11-slide deck measured at ~5–7 min vs 20-min slot, three substance-based gap-closure options framed. Versioning/bug-fix session (01:29): deck expanded to 14 slides (v2), original preserved as v1, slide-8 chart overflow fixed with `aspect-ratio` + `max-height: min(40vh, 320px)`.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/baylor-load-simulator-deployment.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Baylor Load Simulator: Vercel Deployment and Navigation\"\naliases: [Baylor Load Simulator, simulator app, GPS Sync admin panel, Reference Card page, vercel link, CATAPULT_TOKEN env var, Comparison View]\ntags: [vercel, nextjs, neon, deployment, baylor-athletics, catapult]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-24.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-05-12.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-14\nupdated: 2026-06-15\n---\n\n# Baylor Load Simulator: Vercel Deployment and Navigation\n\nThe Baylor Load Simulator app was deployed to Vercel production on 2026-04-14, fixing a server/client date-serialization bug along the way and adding persistent navigation between its `/reference` (Reference Card) and `/admin` (GPS Sync) pages. Active feature development continued into May 2026, including a Projected-vs-Actual comparison view and ongoing work to reconcile the app's data model with Catapult's real-world sync behavior.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Neon (serverless Postgres) returns `practice_date` and `synced_at` columns as native JS `Date` objects rather than strings; these had to be serialized to ISO strings in the API routes before being passed to React client components.\n- The Vercel project was not yet linked, so the MCP deploy tool could not deploy directly - `npx vercel link` had to be run interactively in a separate terminal first, as a one-time prerequisite.\n- The `CATAPULT_TOKEN` value could not be pasted directly into chat (likely due to special characters); the workaround was to add it to `.env.local`, then have the assistant read the file and push the value to Vercel via the CLI.\n- A persistent dark navigation bar was added to the authenticated layout, linking to the previously-unlinked `/reference` and `/admin` pages; it is hidden on `/login` and visible on all authenticated pages. A third top-level tab, `/comparison`, was planned on 2026-05-12 to surface the new Projected-vs-Actual comparison view (see [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-comparison-view]]).\n- `.vercel/` remains gitignored per standard practice (the project link itself is not committed), and `CLAUDE.md` was updated with the simulator's architecture, known gotchas, and deploy commands for future sessions.\n\n## Details\n\nThe Neon date-serialization issue is a reusable lesson beyond this one app: any Next.js project querying Neon (or other Postgres providers using the `pg`/`@neondatabase/serverless` drivers) should expect timestamp/date columns to come back as JS `Date` objects, which are not directly serializable across the server-to-client component boundary. The fix - explicitly converting these fields to ISO strings inside API routes before they reach client components - resolved the deployment-blocking error.\n\nTwo pages, the Reference Card (`/reference`) and GPS Sync (`/admin`), existed in the codebase but had no way to reach them from the UI; adding the nav bar made them discoverable. The GPS Sync panel is functionally complete but not yet useful in practice - it will only become \"live\" once Catapult has actual GPS data for the practice dates being tested, which ties this deployment to the broader, ongoing challenge of getting reliable Catapult data into Baylor Athletics' various tools. Outstanding work includes connecting GitHub auto-deploy (Vercel dashboard, Settings > Git, with root directory set to `simulator`) so future pushes to `main` deploy automatically without a manual CLI step.\n\nA month later, on 2026-05-12, development resumed with a `ComparisonChart` feature overlaying projected vs. actual practice load on `/practices/[id]/compare`, plus a confirmed plan to promote it to a top-level `/comparison` nav tab (see [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-comparison-view]]). Building that chart surfaced a Catapult sync-aggregation issue - the same period type (\"1 ON 1\") appearing twice, non-adjacently, in a single practice's data - which the team chose to leave aggregated for now rather than redesign the sync model mid-season (see [[concepts/catapult-period-aggregation-sync-policy]]). This is the same recurring theme as the GPS Sync panel's dependency on Catapult data: the app's data model and Catapult's actual data don't always line up cleanly, and reconciling them is an ongoing, incremental process rather than a one-time fix.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/keystone-os-tezino-project]] - A separate Baylor Athletics project that also depends on working Catapult API access; see [[connections/catapult-token-friction-across-baylor-projects]] for how the two relate.\n- [[concepts/catapult-connect-api-reference]] - The local Catapult Connect v6 API reference (compiled 2026-06-10) documents the token model (`CATAPULT_TOKEN`/OpenField tokens) that this deployment's GPS Sync panel depends on.\n- [[concepts/jinja2-none-safety-pattern]] - A later (2026-04-24) project hit an analogous framework-quirk gotcha (Jinja2's `default()` filter not reliably handling `None`), fixed the same way this deployment's Neon date-serialization issue was: with an explicit defensive check rather than relying on default framework behavior.\n- [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-comparison-view]] - The 2026-05-12 Projected-vs-Actual comparison feature and planned `/comparison` nav tab built on top of this deployment's navigation structure.\n- [[concepts/catapult-period-aggregation-sync-policy]] - The 2026-05-12 Catapult period-ordering issue uncovered while building the comparison view, a data-layer counterpart to this article's Catapult-token discussion.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] - Vercel deployment session: date serialization fix, vercel link requirement, CATAPULT_TOKEN handling, and nav bar addition.\n- [[daily/2026-04-24.md]] - Source of the analogous Jinja2 None-handling gotcha referenced in [[concepts/jinja2-none-safety-pattern]].\n- [[daily/2026-05-12.md]] - Comparison chart feature, Catapult period-aggregation sync findings, and nav restructuring plan.\n\n```\n\nThese are the 13 articles (of 285) most related to this log, plus graph neighbours and least-linked articles. This is the relevant context, not a partial view to be cautious about.\n- The Current Wiki Index above lists EVERY article. Consult it before creating a new one, and you may [[wikilink]] any article listed there even if its text is not shown.\n- If this log adds detail, correction, or a new example to an article shown above, UPDATE it: re-emit it at its existing path with the full revised content. Updating existing articles is a primary expected outcome, not a fallback.\n\n## Daily Log to Compile\n\n**File:** 2026-09-21.md\n\n# Daily Log: 2026-09-21\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Memory Flush (15:25)\n\nFLUSH_OK - Nothing worth saving from this session\n\n### Session (18:30)\n\n**Context:** Eric moved all three data pulls (GPS, Perch/PlayerProfiles, VALD/ForcePlate) from the Windows laptop to the Mac, then ran closeout and verified the first afternoon scheduled runs.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Windows verification step: after `git pull` in `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\_INBOX`, `Select-String -Path refresh_all_data.bat -Pattern \"skipped\"` must return 3 lines (ForcePlate, GPS, Perch). Eric confirmed done.\n- \"Can I turn VALD off on Windows?\" — already off; the disabled step 1 in `refresh_all_data.bat` IS the VALD step, and Windows VALD had been broken since 8/2 anyway.\n- Afternoon receipt check: all 3 pulls finished exit 0, but Perch and VALD ran ~1 hour late.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- **Skip rotating the exposed VALD client secret** — repo is private and pulls work. Noted as \"skip for now\" in `_INBOX/docs/STATUS.md`.\n- Mac owns all 3 pulls; Windows scheduled task still runs at 8 PM but pulls nothing.\n- Schedule: GPS 10:00/16:30, Perch 10:05/16:35, VALD 10:10/16:40.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **Mac sleep is the real failure mode for scheduled pulls.** The Mac slept at 3:45 PM; GPS ran on time at 4:32, but Perch and VALD only crept forward during short background wakes and finished ~5:36–5:39 PM. Perch timed out twice before try 3 succeeded. Same root cause as the 6:30 AM GPS problem.\n- Data still arrived — retries saved it — but lateness is not reliable.\n- No failure alerts fired, so Perch/VALD alert paths remain unproven.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- **Open decision:** set a daily wake with `sudo pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRFSU 16:28:00` (recommended) vs. leave it. Not set yet. Undo is `sudo pmset repeat cancel`.\n- Perch and VALD failure alerts still untested.\n- Eric can delete the unused GPS database copy: `FA2026/_Mark_for_Deletion/gps_db_handoff_2026-09-21/` on Drive.\n- Handoff written to `~/.claude/global-memory/handoffs/2026-09-21-mac-owns-data-pulls.md`; STATUS.md updated in `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`, `PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026`, `ForcePlate_DecisionSystem`, `_INBOX`.\n\n\n\n## Your Task\n\nDo NOT use any tools and do NOT attempt to write files — your sandbox cannot\nwrite under this directory. Instead, emit the articles as plain text using the\nexact delimiter format below. The orchestrator parses your output and writes the\nfiles itself.\n\n### Output format\n\nFor each new or updated article, emit a block:\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: complete YAML frontmatter + body, per the AGENTS.md schema>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nUse `connections/<slug>.md` for connection articles and `qa/<slug>.md` for Q&A.\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated. To UPDATE an existing article, emit it with\nits existing path and the full revised content (it overwrites).\n\nAfter the article blocks, emit exactly one index block and one log block:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/<slug>]] | one-line summary | 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-22 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-09-22T20:01:36-05:00] compile | 2026-09-21.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-21.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/<slug>]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n===END LOG===\n\n### Rules\n1. Identify 3-7 distinct concepts worth their own article.\n2. Emit ===NOTHING=== ONLY when the log itself contains no durable knowledge\n   (e.g. \"FLUSH_OK\", or purely transient chatter). \"An article already covers\n   this topic\" is NOT a reason to emit ===NOTHING=== - it is a reason to UPDATE\n   that article with whatever this log adds.\n3. Every article: complete YAML frontmatter (title, sources, created, updated)\n   with `sources` citing 2026-09-21.md; at least 2 [[wikilinks]]; Key Points\n   3-5 bullets; Details 2+ paragraphs; Related Concepts 2+ entries; encyclopedia\n   style — neutral and comprehensive. Follow the AGENTS.md article format exactly.\n4. Create a connection article when the log reveals a non-obvious relationship\n   between 2+ concepts.\n4b. Do NOT re-emit an article whose only change would be the `updated:` date or\n   a restatement of what it already says. Measured on a real run, 4 of 5\n   \"updates\" changed nothing but the timestamp - that is churn: it rewrites\n   files, dirties backups, and invalidates caches while adding no knowledge.\n   Re-emit an article only when you are adding substantive content.\n5. Add one ===INDEX=== table row per article (new or updated).\n\nEmit ONLY the delimiter blocks — no preamble, no commentary.\n"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"dequeue","timestamp":"2026-09-23T01:01:37.568Z","sessionId":"0f7349ea-34f8-497e-96cb-fb7edaa4ed54"}
{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":false,"promptId":"5a1d97ec-97c0-4756-9ffa-89f6fb06d37c","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are a knowledge compiler. Your job is to read a daily conversation log\nand extract knowledge into structured wiki articles.\n\n## Schema (AGENTS.md)\n\n# AGENTS.md - Personal Knowledge Base Schema\n\n> Adapted from [Andrej Karpathy's LLM Knowledge Base](https://gist.github.com/karpathy/442a6bf555914893e9891c11519de94f) architecture.\n> Instead of ingesting external articles, this system compiles knowledge from your own AI conversations.\n\n## The Compiler Analogy\n\n```\ndaily/          = source code    (your conversations - the raw material)\nLLM             = compiler       (extracts and organizes knowledge)\nknowledge/      = executable     (structured, queryable knowledge base)\nlint            = test suite     (health checks for consistency)\nmemlint         = test suite     (same, for the memory files loaded each session)\nqueries         = runtime        (using the knowledge)\n```\n\nYou don't manually organize your knowledge. You have conversations, and the LLM handles the synthesis, cross-referencing, and maintenance.\n\n---\n\n## Architecture\n\n### Layer 1: `daily/` - Conversation Logs (Immutable Source)\n\nDaily logs capture what happened in your AI coding sessions. These are the \"raw sources\" - append-only, never edited after the fact.\n\n```\ndaily/\n├── 2026-04-01.md\n├── 2026-04-02.md\n├── ...\n```\n\nEach file follows this format:\n\n```markdown\n# Daily Log: YYYY-MM-DD\n\n## Sessions\n\n### Session (HH:MM) - Brief Title\n\n**Context:** What the user was working on.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- User asked about X, assistant explained Y\n- Decided to use Z approach because...\n- Discovered that W doesn't work when...\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Chose library X over Y because...\n- Architecture: went with pattern Z\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Always do X before Y to avoid...\n- The gotcha with Z is that...\n\n**Action Items:**\n- [ ] Follow up on X\n- [ ] Refactor Y when time permits\n```\n\n### Layer 2: `knowledge/` - Compiled Knowledge (LLM-Owned)\n\nThe LLM owns this directory entirely. Humans read it but rarely edit it directly.\n\n```\nknowledge/\n├── index.md              # Master catalog - every article with one-line summary\n├── log.md                # Append-only chronological build log\n├── concepts/             # Atomic knowledge articles\n├── connections/          # Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n└── qa/                   # Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n```\n\n### Layer 3: This File (AGENTS.md)\n\nThe schema that tells the LLM how to compile and maintain the knowledge base. This is the \"compiler specification.\"\n\n---\n\n## Structural Files\n\n### `knowledge/index.md` - Master Catalog\n\nA table listing every knowledge article. This is the primary retrieval mechanism - the LLM reads this FIRST when answering any query, then selects relevant articles to read in full.\n\nFormat:\n\n```markdown\n# Knowledge Base Index\n\n| Article | Summary | Compiled From | Updated |\n|---------|---------|---------------|---------|\n| [[concepts/supabase-auth]] | Row-level security patterns and JWT gotchas | daily/2026-04-02.md | 2026-04-02 |\n| [[connections/auth-and-webhooks]] | Token verification patterns shared across Supabase auth and Stripe webhooks | daily/2026-04-02.md, daily/2026-04-04.md | 2026-04-04 |\n```\n\n### `knowledge/log.md` - Build Log\n\nAppend-only chronological record of every compile, query, and lint operation.\n\nFormat:\n\n```markdown\n# Build Log\n\n## [2026-04-01T14:30:00] compile | Daily Log 2026-04-01\n- Source: daily/2026-04-01.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/nextjs-project-structure]], [[concepts/tailwind-setup]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n\n## [2026-04-02T09:00:00] query | \"How do I handle auth redirects?\"\n- Consulted: [[concepts/supabase-auth]], [[concepts/nextjs-middleware]]\n- Filed to: [[qa/auth-redirect-handling]]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Article Formats\n\n### Concept Articles (`knowledge/concepts/`)\n\nOne article per atomic piece of knowledge. These are facts, patterns, decisions, preferences, and lessons extracted from your conversations.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Concept Name\"\naliases: [alternate-name, abbreviation]\ntags: [domain, topic]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-01.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-03.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-01\nupdated: 2026-04-03\n---\n\n# Concept Name\n\n[2-4 sentence core explanation]\n\n## Key Points\n\n- [Bullet points, each self-contained]\n\n## Details\n\n[Deeper explanation, encyclopedia-style paragraphs]\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/related-concept]] - How it connects\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-01.md]] - Initial discovery during project setup\n- [[daily/2026-04-03.md]] - Updated after debugging session\n```\n\n### Connection Articles (`knowledge/connections/`)\n\nCross-cutting synthesis linking 2+ concepts. Created when a conversation reveals a non-obvious relationship.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: X and Y\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/concept-x\"\n  - \"concepts/concept-y\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-04.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-04\nupdated: 2026-04-04\n---\n\n# Connection: X and Y\n\n## The Connection\n\n[What links these concepts]\n\n## Key Insight\n\n[The non-obvious relationship discovered]\n\n## Evidence\n\n[Specific examples from conversations]\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/concept-x]]\n- [[concepts/concept-y]]\n```\n\n### Q&A Articles (`knowledge/qa/`)\n\nFiled answers from queries. Every complex question answered by the system can be permanently stored, making future queries smarter.\n\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Q: Original Question\"\nquestion: \"The exact question asked\"\nconsulted:\n  - \"concepts/article-1\"\n  - \"concepts/article-2\"\nfiled: 2026-04-05\n---\n\n# Q: Original Question\n\n## Answer\n\n[The synthesized answer with [[wikilinks]] to sources]\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- [[concepts/article-1]] - Relevant because...\n- [[concepts/article-2]] - Provided context on...\n\n## Follow-Up Questions\n\n- What about edge case X?\n- How does this change if Y?\n```\n\n---\n\n## Core Operations\n\n### 1. Compile (daily/ -> knowledge/)\n\nWhen processing a daily log:\n\n1. Read the daily log file\n2. Read `knowledge/index.md` to understand current knowledge state\n3. Read existing articles that may need updating\n4. For each piece of knowledge found in the log:\n   - If an existing concept article covers this topic: UPDATE it with new information, add the daily log as a source\n   - If it's a new topic: CREATE a new `concepts/` article\n5. If the log reveals a non-obvious connection between 2+ existing concepts: CREATE a `connections/` article\n6. UPDATE `knowledge/index.md` with new/modified entries\n7. APPEND to `knowledge/log.md`\n\n**Important guidelines:**\n- A single daily log may touch 3-10 knowledge articles\n- Prefer updating existing articles over creating near-duplicates\n- Use Obsidian-style `[[wikilinks]]` with full relative paths from knowledge/\n- Write in encyclopedia style - factual, concise, self-contained\n- Every article must have YAML frontmatter\n- Every article must link back to its source daily logs\n\n### 2. Query (Ask the Knowledge Base)\n\n1. Read `knowledge/index.md` (the master catalog)\n2. Based on the question, identify 3-10 relevant articles from the index\n3. Read those articles in full\n4. Synthesize an answer with `[[wikilink]]` citations\n5. If `--file-back` is specified: create a `knowledge/qa/` article and update index.md and log.md\n\n**Why this works without RAG:** At personal knowledge base scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms cosine similarity. The LLM understands what the question is really asking and selects pages accordingly. Embeddings find similar words; the LLM finds relevant concepts.\n\n### 3. Lint (Health Checks)\n\nSeven checks, run periodically:\n\n1. **Broken links** - `[[wikilinks]]` pointing to non-existent articles\n2. **Orphan pages** - Articles with zero inbound links from other articles\n3. **Orphan sources** - Daily logs that haven't been compiled yet\n4. **Stale articles** - Source daily log changed since article was last compiled\n5. **Contradictions** - Conflicting claims across articles (requires LLM judgment)\n6. **Missing backlinks** - A links to B but B doesn't link back to A\n7. **Sparse articles** - Below 200 words, likely incomplete\n\nOutput: a markdown report with severity levels (error, warning, suggestion).\n\n---\n\n## Conventions\n\n- **Wikilinks:** Use Obsidian-style `[[path/to/article]]` without `.md` extension\n- **Writing style:** Encyclopedia-style, factual, third-person where appropriate\n- **Dates:** ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD for dates, full ISO for timestamps in log.md)\n- **File naming:** lowercase, hyphens for spaces (e.g., `supabase-row-level-security.md`)\n- **Frontmatter:** Every article must have YAML frontmatter with at minimum: title, sources, created, updated\n- **Sources:** Always link back to the daily log(s) that contributed to an article\n\n---\n\n## Full Project Structure\n\n```\nllm-personal-kb/\n|-- .claude/\n|   |-- settings.json                # Hook configuration (auto-activates in Claude Code)\n|-- .gitignore                       # Excludes runtime state, temp files, caches\n|-- AGENTS.md                        # This file - schema + full technical reference\n|-- README.md                        # Concise overview + quick start\n|-- pyproject.toml                   # Dependencies (at root so hooks can find it)\n|-- daily/                           # \"Source code\" - conversation logs (immutable)\n|-- knowledge/                       # \"Executable\" - compiled knowledge (LLM-owned)\n|   |-- index.md                     #   Master catalog - THE retrieval mechanism\n|   |-- log.md                       #   Append-only build log\n|   |-- concepts/                    #   Atomic knowledge articles\n|   |-- connections/                 #   Cross-cutting insights linking 2+ concepts\n|   |-- qa/                          #   Filed query answers (compounding knowledge)\n|-- scripts/                         # CLI tools\n|   |-- compile.py                   #   Compile daily logs -> knowledge articles\n|   |-- query.py                     #   Ask questions (index-guided, no RAG)\n|   |-- lint.py                      #   7 health checks (knowledge base)\n|   |-- memlint.py                   #   6 health checks (memory tier)\n|   |-- flush.py                     #   Extract memories from conversations (background)\n|   |-- config.py                    #   Path constants\n|   |-- utils.py                     #   Shared helpers\n|-- hooks/                           # Claude Code hooks\n|   |-- session-start.py             #   Injects knowledge into every session\n|   |-- session-end.py               #   Extracts conversation -> daily log\n|   |-- pre-compact.py               #   Safety net: captures context before compaction\n|-- reports/                         # Lint reports (gitignored)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Hook System (Automatic Capture)\n\nHooks are configured in `.claude/settings.json` and fire automatically when you use Claude Code in this project.\n\n### `.claude/settings.json` Format\n\n```json\n{\n  \"hooks\": {\n    \"SessionStart\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-start.py\", \"timeout\": 15 }] }],\n    \"PreCompact\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/pre-compact.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }],\n    \"SessionEnd\": [{ \"matcher\": \"\", \"hooks\": [{ \"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"uv run python hooks/session-end.py\", \"timeout\": 10 }] }]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCommands use simple relative paths from the project root. Empty `matcher` catches all events.\n\n### Hook Details\n\n**`session-start.py`** (SessionStart)\n- Pure local I/O, no API calls, runs in under 1 second\n- Reads `knowledge/index.md` and the most recent daily log\n- Outputs JSON to stdout: `{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"...\"}}`\n- Claude sees the knowledge base index at the start of every session\n- Max context: 20,000 characters\n\n**`session-end.py`** (SessionEnd)\n- Reads hook input from stdin (JSON with `session_id`, `transcript_path`, `cwd`)\n- Copies the raw JSONL transcript to a temp file (no parsing in the hook - keeps it fast)\n- Spawns `flush.py` as a fully detached background process\n- Recursion guard: exits immediately if `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY` env var is set\n\n**`pre-compact.py`** (PreCompact)\n- Same architecture as session-end.py\n- Fires before Claude Code auto-compacts the context window\n- Guards against empty `transcript_path` (known Claude Code bug #13668)\n- Critical for long sessions: captures context before summarization discards it\n\n**Why both PreCompact and SessionEnd?** Long-running sessions may trigger multiple auto-compactions before you close the session. Without PreCompact, intermediate context is lost to summarization before SessionEnd ever fires.\n\n### Background Flush Process (`flush.py`)\n\nSpawned by both hooks as a fully detached background process:\n- **Windows:** `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP | DETACHED_PROCESS` flags\n- **Mac/Linux:** `start_new_session=True`\n\nThis ensures flush.py survives after Claude Code's hook process exits.\n\n**What flush.py does:**\n1. Sets `CLAUDE_INVOKED_BY=memory_flush` env var (prevents recursive hook firing)\n2. Reads the pre-extracted conversation context from the temp `.md` file\n3. Skips if context is empty or if same session was flushed within 60 seconds (deduplication)\n4. Calls Claude Agent SDK (`query()` with `allowed_tools=[]`, `max_turns=2`)\n5. Claude decides what's worth saving - returns structured bullet points or `FLUSH_OK`\n6. Appends result to `daily/YYYY-MM-DD.md`\n7. Cleans up temp context file\n8. **End-of-day auto-compilation:** If it's past 6 PM local time (`COMPILE_AFTER_HOUR = 18`) and today's daily log has changed since its last compilation (hash comparison against `state.json`), spawns `compile.py` as another detached background process. This means compilation happens automatically once a day without needing a cron job or manual trigger.\n\n### JSONL Transcript Format\n\nClaude Code stores conversations as `.jsonl` files. Messages are nested under a `message` key:\n\n```python\nentry = json.loads(line)\nmsg = entry.get(\"message\", {})\nrole = msg.get(\"role\", \"\")     # \"user\" or \"assistant\"\ncontent = msg.get(\"content\", \"\")  # string or list of content blocks\n```\n\nContent can be a string or a list of blocks (`{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"...\"}` dicts).\n\n---\n\n## Script Details\n\n### compile.py - The Compiler\n\nUses the Claude Agent SDK's async streaming `query()`:\n\n```python\nasync for message in query(\n    prompt=compile_prompt,\n    options=ClaudeAgentOptions(\n        cwd=str(ROOT_DIR),\n        system_prompt={\"type\": \"preset\", \"preset\": \"claude_code\"},\n        allowed_tools=[\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Edit\", \"Glob\", \"Grep\"],\n        permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\",\n        max_turns=30,\n    ),\n):\n```\n\n- Builds a prompt with: AGENTS.md schema, current index, all existing articles, and the daily log\n- Claude reads the daily log, decides what concepts to extract, and writes files directly\n- `permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\"` auto-approves all file operations\n- Incremental: tracks SHA-256 hashes of daily logs in `state.json`, skips unchanged files\n- Cost: ~$0.45-0.65 per daily log (increases as KB grows)\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/compile.py              # compile new/changed only\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --all        # force recompile everything\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --file daily/2026-04-01.md\nuv run python scripts/compile.py --dry-run\n```\n\n### query.py - Index-Guided Retrieval\n\nLoads the entire knowledge base into context (index + all articles). No RAG.\n\nAt personal KB scale (50-500 articles), the LLM reading a structured index outperforms vector similarity. The LLM understands what you're really asking; cosine similarity just finds similar words.\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/query.py \"What auth patterns do I use?\"\nuv run python scripts/query.py \"What's my error handling strategy?\" --file-back\n```\n\nWith `--file-back`, creates a Q&A article in `knowledge/qa/` and updates the index and log. This is the compounding loop - every question makes the KB smarter.\n\n### lint.py - Health Checks\n\nSeven checks:\n\n| Check | Type | Catches |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Broken links | Structural | `[[wikilinks]]` to non-existent articles |\n| Orphan pages | Structural | Articles with zero inbound links |\n| Orphan sources | Structural | Daily logs not yet compiled |\n| Stale articles | Structural | Source logs changed since compilation |\n| Missing backlinks | Structural | A links to B but B doesn't link back |\n| Sparse articles | Structural | Under 200 words |\n| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting claims across articles |\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/lint.py                    # all checks\nuv run python scripts/lint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\n```\n\nReports saved to `reports/lint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`.\n\n### memlint.py - Memory Tier Health Checks\n\n`lint.py` stops at `knowledge/`. The files the harness actually injects into every\nsession live elsewhere and were never audited: `~/.claude/global-memory/` and the\nactive `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/` slugs. Drift there is the most expensive\ndrift in the system, because it is read at the start of every conversation.\n\nSeven checks:\n\n| Check | Type | Catches |\n|-------|------|---------|\n| Index drift | Structural | Memory file missing from MEMORY.md, or an index pointer with no file |\n| Broken links | Structural | `[[links]]` that do not match any frontmatter `name:` in the slug |\n| Name form | Structural | `name:` that is not the bare kebab stem (type belongs in `metadata.type`) |\n| Near-duplicates | Structural | Within-slug TF-IDF similarity above the threshold |\n| Cap overruns | Structural | A file past the character cap it declares in its own header |\n| Content in index | Structural | Facts written into MEMORY.md, which is meant to hold pointers |\n| Contradictions | LLM | Conflicting, redundant, or superseded claims across memory files |\n\n**Scope.** Retired `C--Users-eric-rash*` slugs are excluded everywhere - the macOS\nslugs were re-keyed from them, so including them reports history as duplication.\n`handoffs/` and `transcripts/` are session records, not memory, and are never linted.\nThe LLM check defaults to `global-memory` + the `DEV` and `~` slugs, the ones that\nload together in a working session; `--all-slugs` widens it.\n\n**Similarity threshold.** Default 0.45. Measured 2026-09-01: the highest genuine\nwithin-slug pair scored 0.33. The report always prints the top pairs per slug so\nthe number can be retuned against evidence.\n\n**Report only.** memlint never edits a memory file. Merging and deleting memories\nstays a human decision.\n\n**CLI:**\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py                    # all checks\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --structural-only  # skip LLM check (free)\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --all-slugs        # widen the LLM check\nuv run python scripts/memlint.py --threshold 0.6    # retune near-duplicates\n```\n\nReports saved to `reports/memlint-YYYY-MM-DD.md`; `--structural-only` writes\n`memlint-YYYY-MM-DD-structural.md` instead, so a free run never clobbers the\nday's paid-for LLM findings. Runs automatically after the Sunday 02:30\nadjudication job.\n\n---\n\n## State Tracking\n\n`scripts/state.json` tracks:\n- `ingested` - map of daily log filenames to SHA-256 hashes, compilation timestamps, and costs\n- `query_count` - total queries run\n- `last_lint` - timestamp of most recent lint\n- `last_memlint` - timestamp of most recent memory-tier lint\n- `total_cost` - cumulative API cost\n\n`scripts/last-flush.json` tracks flush deduplication (session_id + timestamp).\n\nBoth are gitignored and regenerated automatically.\n\n---\n\n## Dependencies\n\n`pyproject.toml` (at project root):\n- `claude-agent-sdk>=0.1.29` - Claude Agent SDK for LLM calls with tool use\n- `python-dotenv>=1.0.0` - Environment variable management\n- `tzdata>=2024.1` - Timezone data\n- Python 3.12+, managed by [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/)\n\nNo API key needed - uses Claude Code's built-in credentials at `~/.claude/.credentials.json`.\n\n---\n\n## Costs\n\n| Operation | Cost |\n|-----------|------|\n| Compile one daily log | $0.45-0.65 |\n| Query (no file-back) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Query (with file-back) | ~$0.25-0.40 |\n| Full lint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Structural lint only | $0.00 |\n| Full memlint (with contradictions) | ~$0.15-0.25 |\n| Structural memlint only | $0.00 |\n| Memory flush (per session) | ~$0.02-0.05 |\n\n---\n\n## Customization\n\n### Additional Article Types\n\nAdd directories like `people/`, `projects/`, `tools/` to `knowledge/`. Define the article format in this file (AGENTS.md) and update `utils.py`'s `list_wiki_articles()` to include them.\n\n### Obsidian Integration\n\nThe knowledge base is pure markdown with `[[wikilinks]]` - works natively in Obsidian. Point a vault at `knowledge/` for graph view, backlinks, and search.\n\n### Scaling Beyond Index-Guided Retrieval\n\nAt ~2,000+ articles / ~2M+ tokens, the index becomes too large for the context window. At that point, add hybrid RAG (keyword + semantic search) as a retrieval layer before the LLM. See Karpathy's recommendation of `qmd` by Tobi Lutke for search at scale.\n\n\n## Current Wiki Index\n\n# Knowledge Base Index\n\n| Article | Summary | Compiled From | Updated |\n|---------|---------|---------------|---------|\n| [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] | Adds 2026-07-09 GitHub MCP 401-on-reconnect: configured Authorization header disables OAuth fallback; fix is token refresh or header removal | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-03.md, 2026-06-09.md, 2026-07-09.md | 2026-08-01 |\n| [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]] | Three new skills (web-scraping, ingest-resource, improve-system) built for the SecondBrain vault; now cross-linked to an earlier Obsidian-vault-as-destination decision | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/daily-onramp-automation]] | Lean-mode on-ramp pipeline; now includes 2026-06-05 sparse-input run and cumulative ctx savings (4.0 MB / 22 days / 105 conversations, 214× compression before /compact) | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-05.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[concepts/catapult-connect-api-reference]] | Local Catapult Connect v6 API reference; now cross-linked to a mirror-image Perch API auth-scheme lesson from 2026-04-25 | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/keystone-os-tezino-project]] | KeystoneOS Tezino Phase 1 data-gap closure (CMJ/IMTP backfill, mRSI decline, Catapult token failure); now cross-linked to downstream Tezino_CampPrep SPC monitoring project initiated 2026-06-09 | 2026-06-10.md, 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-25.md, 2026-06-09.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[connections/catapult-api-docs-and-tezino-token-issue]] | Same-day link between the Catapult API reference's token docs and the Tezino sync's stale-token failure | 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/web-scraping-skill-and-readme-doc-trick]] | Gap between the new web-scraping skill's routing tiers and the ReadMe .md/llms.txt technique that actually worked | 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] | Memory compiler setup; now includes version-skew failure mode (claude-agent-sdk bundled CLI diverges from system), fix via uv lock --upgrade-package, and Tier B independence confirmed | 2026-06-13.md | 2026-06-16 |\n| [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-deployment]] | Baylor Load Simulator Vercel deployment; now includes the 2026-05-12 Comparison View feature and Catapult period-aggregation sync findings | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-04-24.md, 2026-05-12.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/skylar-zinnecker-cmj-rtc-reports]] | CMJ return-to-competition HTML reports for athlete Skylar Zinnecker, built from a VALD ForceDecks export, with redefined baseline and \"Post-Op Low\" terminology | 2026-04-14.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/catapult-token-friction-across-baylor-projects]] | Recurring Catapult OpenField token friction linking the Baylor Load Simulator deploy, the Tezino sync failure, and the Catapult API reference | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/vald-forcedecks-cmj-data-across-projects]] | Shared VALD ForceDecks CMJ data and mRSI metric linking Skylar Zinnecker's RTC reports to the Tezino narrative backfill | 2026-04-14.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/sportscience-research-repo-batch-import]] | Round-7 batch import + data-quality repair; now records the \"verify prior background agents actually finished\" procedural lesson from 2026-04-22 | 2026-04-21.md, 2026-04-22.md, 2026-04-24.md | 2026-08-06 |\n| [[concepts/graphify-vault-separation]] | Decision to move graphify's 300+ generated files out of SportScienceResearchRepo and into the user's Obsidian vault | 2026-04-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/obsidian-vault-as-knowledge-hub]] | Obsidian SecondBrain as recurring knowledge destination: graphify cleanup, /ingest-resource skill, morning briefing durable copy, and Applied Performance OKR hub as canonical project home | 2026-04-21.md, 2026-06-10.md, 2026-05-21.md, 2026-06-01.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/the-monitor-newsletter]] | \"The Monitor\" newsletter Issue 01 production: content verification, staff photo integration, preview-server rendering gotcha, and template-directory documentation | 2026-04-22.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] | Links a 2026-04-22 agent-citation-hallucination fix and a newsletter photo-rendering gotcha as instances of \"verify directly, don't trust the intermediate layer\" | 2026-04-22.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/perch-weight-room-domain-feature]] | Perch/Weight Room domain feature, now including its 2026-04-25 data-activation completion (436 athletes, 12,300 set records ingested via corrected Perch API integration) | 2026-04-24.md, 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/subagent-driven-development]] | Two-stage review methodology (spec-compliance then code-quality) used to implement and verify Perch/Weight Room Task 9 | 2026-04-24.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/jinja2-none-safety-pattern]] | Jinja2's `default()` filter unreliably handles `None`; use explicit `is not none` guards instead | 2026-04-24.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/multi-stage-review-catches-agent-blind-spots]] | Multi-stage review principle extended to planning context: spec self-review catching carryover resolution behavioral gap | 2026-04-22.md, 2026-04-24.md, 2026-05-21.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/perch-api-integration-reference]] | Verified Perch API integration details (Bearer auth, /v3/sets pagination, 1RM formula, exercise/org IDs) that completed the Weight Room domain's data-activation step | 2026-04-25.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/api-credential-assumptions-can-be-backwards]] | Links a 2026-04-25 Perch auth-scheme surprise (Bearer worked, JWT didn't) to the 2026-06-10 Catapult token failure as mirror-image \"credential appearance is unreliable\" lessons | 2026-04-25.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-comparison-view]] | Projected-vs-Actual ComparisonChart on /practices/[id]/compare, planned /comparison nav tab, single-metric scope; now cross-linked to the Color Triad Intended↔Realized leg | 2026-05-12.md | 2026-08-06 |\n| [[concepts/catapult-period-aggregation-sync-policy]] | Catapult logs the same period type twice non-adjacently within a practice; aggregation-only sync policy kept for now, plus a \"no GPS data\" wording fix | 2026-05-12.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[connections/catapult-friction-spans-auth-and-data-layers]] | Links the 2026-05-12 Catapult period-aggregation data issue to the 2026-06-10 Catapult token failure as friction at two different integration layers | 2026-05-12.md, 2026-06-10.md | 2026-06-15 |\n| [[concepts/claude-code-cli-marketplace-setup]] | cli-printing-press + GSD + npx skills add installation patterns on Windows, with 87-CLI catalog | 2026-05-13.md | 2026-05-13 |\n| [[concepts/gsd-hook-bash-rewrite]] | GSD generates PowerShell-syntax hooks; Claude Code runs hooks through bash — all 9 required manual rewriting | 2026-05-13.md | 2026-05-13 |\n| [[concepts/windows-git-ssh-https-rewrite]] | Permanent fix for SSH plugin clone failures on Windows: global git URL rewrite from git@github.com to https:// | 2026-05-13.md | 2026-05-13 |\n| [[connections/hook-execution-environment-path-assumptions]] | Links GSD's PowerShell hook mismatch to the claude-memory-compiler's uv PATH issue as the same \"hook env ≠ interactive shell\" failure class | 2026-05-13.md, 2026-04-14.md | 2026-05-13 |\n| [[concepts/zotero-research-library-pp-cli]] | Adds 2026-08-06 publish PR #1668 passing an 8-round Greptile review that hardened the credential store and incremental-sync durability | 2026-05-18.md, 2026-05-19.md, 2026-05-21.md, 2026-08-06.md | 2026-08-06 |\n| [[concepts/zotero-api-integration-reference]] | Eight Zotero API quirks: write token, MultiWriteResponse, 412 optimistic-lock retry, raw-bytes passthrough, file-upload short-circuit, 403/401 distinction, and the If-Modified-Since-Version vs since=N sync trap | 2026-05-18.md | 2026-05-18 |\n| [[concepts/printing-press-cli-slash-commands]] | `/pp-catapult-openfield-connect` v1.0.0 and `/pp-zotero-research-library` v0.1 registered as slash commands; p